TI  - Privacy-Preserving Lung Disease Classification via ResNet50 Feature Extraction and Fully Homomorphic Encryption
PY  - 2025
T2  - 2025 10th International Conference on Applying New Technology in Green Buildings, ATiGB 2025
SP  - 618
EP  - 623
DO  - 10.1109/ATIGB66719.2025.11142158
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105017126599?origin=resultslist
AB  - Maintaining patient privacy while ensuring diagnostic accuracy poses a critical challenge in the advancement of AI applications in healthcare. This study proposes an approach to lung disease classification leveraging Fully Homomorphic Encryption (FHE) through the Concrete-ML framework, enabling secure inference on encrypted data without exposing sensitive medical information. We evaluate the performance of FHE-based inference using two machine learning models: Logistic Regression (LR) and Decision Tree (DT). When applied to encrypted features extracted from chest X-rays, these models achieved classification accuracies of 73% and 51%, respectively. Given the computational overhead associated with FHE, direct processing of raw images and complex models remains impractical. To mitigate this, input images are pre-processed-typically resized using bicubic interpolation-and feature extraction is conducted using convolutional neural networks such as ResNet50 prior to encryption. Results show that while Decision Tree are limited under FHE, Logistic Regression offers a practical balance between privacy and performance. © 2025 IEEE.
