UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105035205642?origin=resultslist
AB  - Human Activity Recognition (HAR) systems, which mainly use sensors to generate data and predict the results using machine learning, are increasingly being used in healthcare, security, and smart home devices. These devices are using the processing power and storage offered by cloud service providers, which exposes them to data privacy risks such as data breaches, re-identification, and surveillance. This paper investigates key privacy challenges in cloud-based HAR systems and discusses the effectiveness of solutions like federated learning, homomorphic encryption, and differential privacy. It analyzes the existing research approaches used by academicians and leading cloud service providers and identifies that adoption of these solutions is essential not just for compliance with regulatory standards but also for maintaining user trust. A HAR system needs to keep a balance between providing efficiency and maintaining the data privacy of the user. © 2025 IEEE.
