T2  - 2026 9th International Conference on Advanced Algorithms and Control Engineering, ICAACE 2026
SP  - 1981
EP  - 1984
DO  - 10.1109/ICAACE69793.2026.11509188
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105041630558?origin=resultslist
AB  - Quantum computing threatens data security in cloud-edge-end architectures. We introduce PQ-CEC, a unified framework integrating post-quantum cryptography (Kyber-512, Dilithium-2), privacy-preserving computation (BFV homomorphic encryption, federated learning), and a DQN-based dynamic task offloading strategy to optimize latency and energy. Evaluated on financial and medical datasets, PQ-CEC achieves NIST PQC Level 2 security with merely an 18.7% overhead increase versus RSA. Against existing post-quantum federated learning baselines, it reduces end-to-end latency by 42.3% and sustains 82% throughput across 100 nodes. Furthermore, membership inference attacks fail (51.3% success rate), confirming robust privacy. PQ-CEC ultimately provides a scalable, efficient architecture balancing forward-looking security with cross-industry collaboration.  © 2026 IEEE.
