TI  - Enhancing Federated Learning Privacy Through Encrypted Computation
PY  - 2026
T2  - Procedia Computer Science
VL  - 283
SP  - 3452
EP  - 3462
DO  - 10.1016/j.procs.2026.06.408
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105046101135?origin=resultslist
AB  - Ensuring patient privacy while leveraging artificial intelligence in healthcare is a critical challenge. This work presents a privacy-preserving federated learning framework for classifying brain tumors from MRI scans. Without disclosing private patient information, the proposed system enables collaborative model training across multiple healthcare institutions. To prevent long-term key compromise and ensure secure transmission of intermediate CNN features, we employ homomorphic encryption using the CKKS scheme with periodic key rotation. Each institution locally trains a lightweight MobileNetV3-Small model and encrypts the extracted features with TenSEAL before transmitting them to a central server for aggregation. Our approach presents a novel encrypted feature sharing mechanism that improves privacy without sacrificing model performance, in contrast to traditional methods that encrypt gradients or model updates. Experimental evaluations on a brain MRI dataset demonstrate that the proposed framework increases the accuracy of models from 84.5% (traditional FL) and 87.1% (LEAF) to 94.1%. It safeguards sensitive healthcare data using full CKKS encryption with key rotation and achieves a 5× compression ratio, reducing communication overhead compared to LEAF's 3×. These results highlight its potential for scalable, secure, and high-performance federated AI in real-world clinical settings. © 2026 The Authors.
