C7  - 118194
DO  - 10.1016/j.measurement.2025.118194
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105010337960?origin=resultslist
AB  - In the realm of electric vehicle (EV) technology, the accurate prediction of battery state-of-health (SoH) is paramount for enhancing vehicle reliability and operational efficiency. However, the sensitive nature of data involved in SoH prediction poses significant privacy challenges. In this paper, we proposed a significant advancement “Homomorphic Encryption (HE) enabled Ensemble Deep Learning with Dynamic Random Vector Functional Link Neural Networks (EDL-DRVFLNN)” for Privacy-Preserving Electric Vehicle (EV) Battery Health Prediction. This study leverages the dynamic adaptability of RVFLN models to capture complex nonlinear system dynamics effectively, whereas ensemble learning augments the prediction robustness and accuracy. The incorporation of Homomorphic Encryption ensures the confidentiality of the sensitive data throughout the prediction process. Security analysis demonstrated that the proposed system ensures privacy protection against threats, including poisoning, evasion, member inference, model inversion, and model extraction attacks, during both the training and inference phases, covering aspects such as training data privacy, model privacy, input privacy, and output. Moreover, we conducted an experimental validation on the NASA Battery dataset obtained from Kaggle, which is supported by the Tenseal package. The results show that the proposed privacy-preserving EDL-DRVFLNN (PP-EDL-DRVFLNN) exhibits the best performance with a minimal RMSE of 0.054 when compared to DNN and PPDNN for SoH prediction on NASA Datasets. © 2025 Elsevier Ltd
