UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105040793086?origin=resultslist
AB  - Large Language Models (LLMs) have demonstrated exceptional capabilities in language understanding and generation but pose significant risks when fine-tuned using sensitive, user-specific data. Conventional methods either compromise privacy or degrade performance due to excessive noise and inefficient encryption schemes. The article introduces the new privacy preserving fine-tuning framework, namely PP-EGA-LLM, which is an alternative to homomorphic encryption system but considers interpretability at the cost of gradient filtering during the federation process. Every client calculates encrypted gradients on their own and sums them up in the framework of Secure Multi-Party Computation without sharing the content of the data. SHAP-based filtering masks sensitive components of gradient before update of a model. On sensitive medical and legal datasets, PP-EGA-LLM is more accurate (93.4%) than baselines based on differentially private and encrypted models and has a much lower leakage risk (MIR: 0.18). The model shows nearly centralized performance and acceptable inference latency on edge devices. We are optimized with utility, privacy and efficiency and thus fit quite well in the real-world application in the privacy sensitive field. The architecture can upgrade cryptographically and is therefore post quantum threat extendible. The study is leading the art of secure and ethical fine-tuning of LLMs without losing the accuracy of the data. © 2026 IEEE.
