UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105035182761?origin=resultslist
AB  - With the deep penetration of artificial intelligence technology into the medical field, model training based on multisource medical data has become a core path to improve the accuracy of disease diagnosis and optimize the efficiency of treatment decisions. However, medical data is both highly sensitive and geographically dispersed across institutions. Sharing raw data can easily lead to privacy leaks. Traditional centralized machine learning struggles to balance the core contradiction between 'data value mining' and 'privacy protection,' a pain point that has driven the rapid development of privacy computing technology. Federated learning, as a typical privacy-preserving computation method, protects medical data privacy to a certain extent by sharing model parameters instead of raw data. However, local model parameters in federated learning pose a risk of sensitive information leakage. To address this issue, this paper proposes a lightweight Paillier-LE homomorphic encryption algorithm to resolve the contradiction between local model parameter privacy leakage and excessive homomorphic encryption time. Based on the Paillier algorithm and the Chinese Remainder Theorem, the algorithm is optimized to shorten encryption time while ensuring security and homomorphic properties. Furthermore, gradient pruning techniques are used to further reduce communication overhead. Comparative experiments with two datasets and three other encryption algorithms verify the algorithm's comprehensive advantages in encryption/decryption efficiency and model accuracy. © 2025 IEEE.
