AU  - Eroglu, G.
TI  - Privacy-preserving dyslexia detection using homomorphic encryption for QEEG data with explainable AI insights
PY  - 2026
T2  - Journal of Supercomputing
VL  - 82
IS  - 12
C7  - 633
DO  - 10.1007/s11227-026-08768-z
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105047340701?origin=resultslist
AB  - Privacy concerns surrounding patient data pose major challenges when deploying machine learning (ML) models in cloud environments. To address these issues, this study proposes a homomorphic encryption (HE) inference framework for artificial neural networks (ANNs) that preserves data confidentiality. Because nonlinear activation functions within ANNs can be difficult to handle in encrypted form, we adopt an ANN-based estimator to approximate these nonlinearities securely. We apply this framework to quantitative electroencephalography (QEEG) data for dyslexia detection. Our single-run results show an encrypted inference accuracy of 98.92% and an area under the curve (AUC) of 0.9946. Compared to the plaintext-HE pipeline baseline (accuracy of 99.36%), this represents a marginal 0.44% reduction in classification accuracy with identical AUC performance. This minimal variance demonstrates the feasibility of preserving privacy while maintaining stable predictive performance. The substantial computational overhead of executing approximated activations and matrix multiplications on homomorphically encrypted QEEG data necessitates the use of domain-specific high-performance computing hardware, such as Tensor Processing Units (TPUs), to approach real-time clinical viability. Moreover, a comprehensive conceptual threat model is established to protect against adversarial risks such as model inversion, membership inference, and access-pattern leakage. A key novelty of this work is the integrated focus on privacy-preserving dyslexia detection using QEEG features. In addition, explainability in the encrypted domain is achieved via a perturbation-based SHapley Additive exPlanations (SHAP)-like approximation. By revealing how individual features influence the decision-making process without exposing the underlying data, we advance state-of-the-art approaches for secure, transparent AI in healthcare. © The Author(s), under exclusive licence to Springer Science+Business Media, LLC, part of Springer Nature 2026.
