AB  - AI shows enormous promise in healthcare applications, such as in automated disease diagnoses. Research has found that AI skin cancer identification accuracy is higher than that of doctors, potentially increasing survival rates from 32% to 99%. However, current data protection techniques, such as data sanitization, have known vulnerabilities in AI. Federated learning (FL) has emerged as an increasingly promising framework in privacy-preserving machine learning. FL allows for both limited data sharing and the benefits of AI. Diagnostic errors impact approximately 12 million adults annually in the United States; if AI systems could reduce even 5% of these errors, an estimated 600,000 individuals could benefit. Despite this potential, significant concerns around AI security may limit AI adoption and create legal implications for researchers. Experiments on these threats prove that significant medical information may be leaked from AI models. Up to 99.2% of training data, such as images and tabular data, can be reconstructed based purely on gradients. In response, various defense mechanisms have been tested. These defenses show promise in mitigating specific threats by reducing the extent of information leakage by up to 21.9%. This research highlights the importance of security measures in protecting patient privacy.  © 2025 IEEE.
