AB  - Medical text data used in clinical operations and research often contain Personally Identifiable Information (PII), such as patient names, addresses, dates of birth, phone numbers, insurance, and medical record numbers. Artificial Intelligence techniques, such as machine learning and deep learning, are used to analyze text data containing PII, to identify patterns, and make informed decisions. However, most learning algorithms require access to plaintext during inference, which often exposes the data, making it vulnerable to security breaches. Fully Homomorphic Encryption (FHE) holds potential for secure data analysis; however, it is far from real-world implementation due to its computational intensity. This paper presents a novel framework that integrates TF-IDF with FHE to provide adequate protection of medical PII and privacy. The developed framework was applied to the Extreme Learning Machine (ELM) and Multilayer Perceptron (MLP) neural networks, showing the application of fully homomorphic encryption in neural networks. Four deep learning models were developed, and their performance was evaluated using metrics including accuracy, precision, sensitivity, f1-score, and inference time. Our results analysis shows MLP outperforms ELM across the observed metrics. This study aims to demonstrate the feasibility of FHE with deep learning models in medical text analytics.  © 2026 IEEE.
