TI  - Privacy-Preserving and Scalable Secure Aggregation for Federated Learning in Edge Computing
PY  - 2025
T2  - Proceedings of 2025 2nd International Conference on Cognitive Robotics and Intelligent Systems, ICC - ROBINS 2025
SP  - 182
EP  - 188
DO  - 10.1109/ICC-ROBINS64345.2025.11086126
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105013466651?origin=resultslist
AB  - Federated Learning (FL) has been identified as a leading approach to decentralize machine learning processes, particularly in edge computing contexts where user data privacy is a critical issue. While previous work has made strides in privacypreserving secure aggregation techniques, traditional secure aggregation methods are not scalable, computationally efficient, and robust against attack. Therefore, this work proposes a scalable and Privacy-Preserving Secure Aggregation (PSSA) architecture that advances the onward efficiency and security of FL model updates. The proposed solution provides a solution to robust aggregation through the integration of homomorphic encryption, differential privacy, and adaptive compression to effectively reduce communication overheads. Importantly, in differentiating from prior work, PSSA dynamically updates encryption level based on network conditions: this capability makes PSSA more operationally feasible for resource-constrained edge devices when compared to traditional secure aggregation methods. Based on an extensive evaluation on benchmark datasets, it is found that PSSA achieves and offers up to a 40 % reduced communication cost and 25 % faster aggregation time for FL without sacrificing model accuracy. Finally, based on the theoretical analysis, initial evidence that PSSA is robust against gradient inversion attacks and collusion attacks, has been provided. In enabling efficient, scalable and secure model updates, PSSA advances the practical applicability of federated learning to real-world settings, including health care, cyber security, and smart cities. This work ultimately advances the case for next generation federated learning architectures that prioritizes both secure performance and privacy considerations and the associated deployment in AI-driven toward future edge ecosystems.  © 2025 IEEE.
