DO  - 10.1109/ICMNWC66779.2025.11354261
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105033696119?origin=resultslist
AB  - In modern era, the progress made in artificial intelligence in medicine has changed the way medical images are analyzed, but the privacy and security issues due to centralized collection of patient-sensitive data are huge issues. Traditional deep learning operates on data centralization, violating confidentiality and disrupting collaborative work between medical institutions. This work presents a Federated AttentionEncrypted Federated Learning (FAE-FL) model for medicalimage classification which is privacy-preserving. In this study, the ChestX-ray14 dataset is utilized, distributed over multiple medical nodes, enabling decentralized learning without raw data transfer. The images are pre-processed with normalizing and Contrast Limited Adaptive Histogram Equalization (CLAHE) to suppress the effects of variation in image uniformity and contrast across the medical facilities. An Attention-based ResNet (ARFE) is used for deep feature extraction, which emphasizes parts of the image relevant to the disease, to improve diagnostic accuracy. The FAE-FL model utilizes Partial Homomorphic Encryption (PHE) and Differential Privacy (DP) to provide security of the model updates, combined with Federated Averaging (FedAvg) for the efficiency of global aggregation of the model with cooperation. Experimental results proved the proposed FAE-FL framework achieved better accuracy (98.90%), precision (98.98%), recall (99.67%), and F1-score (98.76%) than exiting Federated Learning (FL) model with significantly reduced encryption overhead, ensuring both high diagnostic performance and strict patient data confidentiality in decentralized healthcare environments.  © 2025 IEEE.
