DO  - 10.1007/978-3-032-28307-8_37
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105047333132?origin=resultslist
AB  - The increasing integration of Artificial Intelligence (AI) and Machine Learning (ML) into healthcare systems has introduced transformative capabilities ranging from early disease diagnosis to predictive analytics. However, this integration is fraught with privacy and security challenges due to the sensitive nature of medical data and growing cyber threats. This paper presents a comprehensive survey and analytical framework for privacy-preserving AI techniques tailored for healthcare applications. We explore and compare prominent approaches including Differential Privacy (DP), Homomorphic Encryption (HE), Secure Multi-Party Computation (SMPC), and Federated Learning (FL). A detailed taxonomy of privacy threats, such as membership inference, model inversion, and data reconstruction attacks, is presented to contextualize the privacy risks in AI-driven systems. We propose a hybrid privacy-preserving architecture combining FL with DP and HE, enabling decentralized model training without sharing raw patient data, while preserving model utility and minimizing privacy leakage. The mathematical foundations of each technique are discussed, and an algorithmic implementation is provided to illustrate real-world applicability. Through comparative analysis, including heat maps, risk matrices, and performance charts, we demonstrate the effectiveness of various techniques across multiple privacy metrics. The findings of this paper underscore the importance of balancing utility and privacy in healthcare AI systems. Our work provides strategic insights for researchers and practitioners aiming to build secure, scalable, and ethically aligned AI solutions in clinical environments. © The Author(s), under exclusive license to Springer Nature Switzerland AG 2026.
