AB  - The flood of smart gadgets connected to the Internet-of-Things (IoT) has led organizations to look for secure machine-learning tools that respect user privacy and can scale on demand. To some extent, Federated learning meets this need because it lets devices train a shared model without sending their raw data to a central server, while blockchain adds a tamper-proof record that everyone can trust. The work presented here combines those ideas into a new framework based on Graph Feature Extracted Sparse-Raven Optimized CNN (GSR-C2N), which was originally created to detect crypto-mining malware. Using GSR-C2N's graph-centric feature grab and fine-tuning engine, the system gains the power to filter IoT traffic and flag threats. Blockchain connects devices together, secures privacy, lets them reach consensus, and keeps a clear audit log. Tests on smart city sensors and healthcare nodes show that this setup achieves 96.85% accuracy and 97.51% specificity using K-fold cross-validation. Finally, scalability runs smoothly, homomorphic encryption keeps sensitive data secure, and everything complies with today's regulations, creating a perfect security blanket for the expanding world of connected things.  © 2025 IEEE.
