TI  - Method for Distributed Data Security Sharing and Recovery of Electric Power Information System
PY  - 2026
T2  - Proceedings of 2026 International Conference on Computer Intelligence and Software Engineering, CICSE 2026
SP  - 28
EP  - 34
DO  - 10.1109/CICSE70286.2026.11620867
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105046978933?origin=resultslist
AB  - With the deep integration of smart grids and the power Internet of Things, power information system data exhibits distributed storage, multi-agent interaction and high sensitivity, making traditional centralized data management unsuitable for secure sharing and reliable recovery. To address data leakage, illegal tampering, node failures and data loss caused by malicious attacks, this paper proposes a secure data sharing and recovery method integrating biostatistical modeling, distributed collaborative optimization, network security protection and AI-enabled cloud computing services. A three-layer wireless sensor network-fog-cloud distributed framework is constructed, where edge devices perform data sensing and preprocessing, fog nodes support collaborative caching and local repair, and cloud platforms provide AI-based node health prediction, access behavior identification and recovery resource scheduling. For secure sharing, consortium blockchain, attribute-based encryption, hash functions and CKKS homomorphic encryption are combined to realize fine-grained access control, encrypted computation and traceable multi-agent data sharing. For reliable recovery, failure probability modeling and survival analysis from biostatistics are introduced to quantify node availability, data survival rate and repair priority. Erasure codes, regenerating codes and an optimal repair tree algorithm are further integrated to optimize distributed redundant storage and data repair paths. Simulation results on typical power multimedia data show that the proposed method reduces synchronization delay and cloud response frequency, improves privacy protection, tamper resistance, fault tolerance and recovery capability, and achieves coordinated optimization of security, reliability and efficiency in distributed smart grid data management. © 2026 IEEE.
