TI  - Secure Chronic Kidney Disease Classification: Homomorphic Encryption vs Differential Privacy in Federated Learning
PY  - 2025
T2  - Proceedings of IEEE International Conference for Women in Innovation, Technology and Entrepreneurship, ICWITE 2025
DO  - 10.1109/ICWITE64848.2025.11306903
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105032357810?origin=resultslist
AB  - The digital transformation of healthcare has transitioned traditional medical records into Electronic Health Records (EHRs), offering rich potential for machine learning applications in disease prediction. However, safeguarding patient privacy remains a critical challenge. Federated Learning (FL) addresses this by enabling collaborative model training across distributed institutions without sharing raw data. In this study, we implement and compare two key privacy-preserving techniques-Homomorphic Encryption (HE) and Differential Privacy (DP)-within an FL framework for predicting Chronic Kidney Disease (CKD). Using data partitioned across multiple clients, we design parallel FL workflows with a unified neural architecture for both approaches. The HE-based system uses TenSEAL for secure encrypted aggregation of model updates, while the DP variant employs the Opacus library to inject calibrated noise during local training. Our results show that HE achieves high predictive accuracy (up to 97.25%) but introduces substantial computational and communication overhead. In contrast, DP offers lower resource consumption and faster convergence, with a reduction in accuracy. This work presents a practical evaluation of privacy-performance trade-offs in FL systems for sensitive healthcare applications and provides guidance for deploying secure, efficient AI in clinical environments.  © 2025 IEEE.
