TI  - Enhancing Healthcare Cybersecurity: A Novel Homomorphic Encryption Framework for Cloud-Based Medical Records
PY  - 2025
T2  - ICCR 2025 - 3rd International Conference on Cyber Resilience
DO  - 10.1109/ICCR67387.2025.11292100
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105031571485?origin=resultslist
AB  - In the modern age of digital healthcare, the confidentiality of the patients' information stored in cloud services is an increasing problem. Earlier methods used for the encryption of data did not provide sufficient privacy while allowing certain manipulations. In this context, this paper presents a new effective solution using homomorphic encryption (HE), a revolutionary method for improving the cybersecurity of cloud-based medical records. Homomorphic encryption can be used in computing on ciphertext to avoid unauthorized access to patients' records while carrying out computations in a cloud environment. Using algorithms such as Parlier and BFV of Brakerski, Fan, and Vaikuntanathan, data within the database can be stored, retrieved, and analyzed securely without decryption. Encrypting the data on the client side before uploading it to the cloud makes it difficult for unauthorized persons to have access to the raw data since they can only unlock it using their passcode. It also allows security querying, which empowers healthcare practitioners to analyze the information and make predictive models from the records without revealing the patient's privacy. Also, it provides data security measures for record authentication and control using digital signatures, as well as access control. It also preserves the security issue and the flexibility and speed characteristics of modern healthcare organizations. In conclusion, this framework is a significant step forward in ensuring that healthcare information security in the cloud is afforded without compromising convenience to legitimate users.  © 2025 IEEE.
