TI  - FedMedNet: A Federated Privacy-Preserving Framework for Multi-Modal EHR-Based Clinical Outcome Prediction
PY  - 2026
T2  - Procedia Computer Science
VL  - 282
SP  - 549
EP  - 558
DO  - 10.1016/j.procs.2026.05.102
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105045568038?origin=resultslist
AB  - Electronic health records (EHRs) contain rich multimodal data which is important in predictive modeling in the medical field. Nevertheless, the data heterogeneity and privacy policies restrict the feasibility of centralized learning in institutions. The proposed study describes FedMedNet that is a federated learning architecture which combines structured clinical information, medical imaging, and textual annotations whilst ensuring patient privacy. The novel architecture of FedMedNet enables healthcare providers to work together and use homomorphic encryption and differential privacy in a secure way. It employs adaptive aggregation with quality and staleness-aware client weighting to support accurate and robust global modeling. Experiments conducted on the MIMIC III and IV datasets show that FedMedNet achieves an AUROC of 0.892 in mortality prediction, performing within 0.6 percent of the centralized baseline and surpassing other federated approaches. Additionally, it demonstrates strong resistance to membership and reconstruction attacks while reducing communication overhead by 38.7 percent. FedMedNet enables real-world deployment of trustworthy and privacy-compliant AI in healthcare without compromising institutional autonomy. © 2026 The Authors.
