AB  - The study presents a privacy-friendly deep learning model, in which homomorphic encryption (HE) is introduced to secure the data confidentiality but does not compromise the performance of the model to the cybersecurity systems on the cloud. Since deep learning has become an essential part of cybersecurity with operations such as threat diagnosis or anomaly analysis, its use in the cloud is associated with fundamental concerns related to privacy since sensitive data may be revealed. The proposed framework solves this problem because it allows processing encrypted data during the lifecycle of the model, using the approximation of polynomial activation functions and training secure methods. The model was tested with benchmark datasets of NSL-KDD and CICIDS2017 regarding its accuracy, latency, scaling properties, and resource consumption. Findings showed that even though the HE-enabled model comes with a moderate overhead in the amount of computation time needed, as well as resource consumption, it has allowed it to attain high accuracy and low privacy leakage. The paper reports that the suggested framework provides a secured, viable and scalable solution to real-time cybersecurity applications in the cloud environment. © The Author(s), under exclusive license to Springer Nature Switzerland AG 2026.
