TI  - A secure multi-modal federated transfer learning system for ECG classification
PY  - 2026
T2  - Neurocomputing
VL  - 681
C7  - 133402
DO  - 10.1016/j.neucom.2026.133402
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105033831156?origin=resultslist
AB  - Federated learning (FL) offers a decentralized approach to model training that preserves data privacy, yet it faces significant challenges, including non-independent and identically distributed (non-IID) data, security vulnerabilities, and high communication costs. Moreover, conventional FL systems typically rely on unimodal data, limiting their ability to capture complementary information across modalities. In this paper, we propose a secure multi-modal federated transfer learning (FTL) framework that jointly leverages electrocardiogram (ECG) signals and images to improve diagnostic performance. Privacy is preserved through homomorphic encryption using the Cheon-Kim-Kim-Song scheme, enabling encrypted model updates and aggregation with minimal utility loss. To the best of our knowledge, we provide the first formal convergence proof for a homomorphically encrypted FTL model trained under differential privacy in a non-IID setting, using the AdamW optimizer. We further present a comprehensive security analysis demonstrating robustness against multiple well-known attacks in an FTL setting. Experimental results on public ECG datasets confirm that our framework achieves high classification accuracy while enhancing privacy, robustness, and scalability. These results underscore the promise of secure multi-modal FTL in advancing privacy-preserving AI for healthcare applications. © 2026 Elsevier B.V.
