DO  - 10.1109/ICCIT68739.2025.11491342
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105041605286?origin=resultslist
AB  - Federated Learning enables collaborative medical research while keeping patient data local. However, transmitting model updates poses a significant risk of gradient leakage. Additionally, standard aggregation methods (e.g., FedAvg) typically imply an average of all clients' learning, treating all clients as equally credible. Hence, they are prone to client drift in heterogeneous, Non-Independent and Identically Distributed environments, where inconsistent or biased updates may degrade global model performance. To solve these challenges, this paper presents a secure, quality-aware federated learning framework that preserves client update confidentiality via homomorphic encryption and enables the server to perform quality-based aggregation over model learning in encrypted form. To address heterogeneous, non-IID data, the framework integrates the standard FSMMG algorithm for variance reduction, achieving 98.77% accuracy on a publicly available brain tumor dataset. These results demonstrate that secure, quality-aware federated learning achieved higher accuracy while preserving data privacy and robustness among heterogeneous clients. © 2025 IEEE.
