SP  - 369
EP  - 374
DO  - 10.1109/ICIRCA69024.2026.11570471
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105044166637?origin=resultslist
AB  - In recent years, the growing importance of digital image data has raised significant privacy concerns, particularly due to the presence of sensitive user information, such as personal and biometric details. Especially the emerging machine learning and deep learning models increasingly rely on vast image datasets for classification and prediction tasks. As a result, protecting image data has become crucial. However, conventional privacy-preserving methods help in securing digital images but they often degrade image utility, making them less effective in deep learning applications. This paper proposes a novel privacy-preserving technique for healthcare images that hides sensitive visual information while preserving their usefulness for deep learning models. The proposed method first extracts key image features, including gradients, edges, contrast and structural information. Based on these features, each pixel is then assigned a feature score. Using additive homomorphic encryption, only the pixels with the highest feature scores, those containing the most critical information are selectively encrypted. The encryption masks sensitive areas without disrupting key visual cues, allowing Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs) to maintain high classification accuracy. It is evident from the experimental results that the CNN classifier achieves nearly comparable accuracy on both encrypted and original images. This indicates that the proposed scheme effectively preserves the privacy of image data without compromising the accuracy of CNN-based predictions. © 2026 IEEE.
