TI  - Examining Federated Learning Architecture's Capability to Protect Data Privacy During the Training of Collaborative Healthcare Models
PY  - 2025
T2  - Proceedings of the 9th International Conference on Inventive Systems and Control, ICISC 2025
SP  - 392
EP  - 398
DO  - 10.1109/ICISC65841.2025.11187468
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105020932629?origin=resultslist
AB  - Working with artificial intelligence (AI) in the healthcare industry yields revolutionary results, especially when machine learning techniques are used to teamwork. Because patient data is sensitive and subject to stringent rules, central data aggregation is difficult. Federated Learning is a computer technique that allows for the decentralised deployment of artificial intelligence by training models at the source device while preserving the original data locally. By analysing healthcare collaborative systems that comprise EHR data, diagnostic imaging data, and wearable health data, researchers assess a variety of FL designs in terms of their ability to maintain privacy or their learning performance. Horizontal FL, Vertical FL, and Hybrid FL are compared based on their communication requirements, training efficiency, time to convergence, and vulnerability to data exposure. Using device simulations on a Raspberry Pi 4 and an NVIDIA Jetson Nano operating under real-world implementation constraints, the study team conducted experimental assessments. To assess real-world applications, the study uses privacy-protected methods such safe aggregation, homomorphic encryption, and differential privacy inside the FL structure. The optimal balance between superior performance as well as privacy protection in simulation operation is achieved by hybrid FL technology that combines adaptive compression with safe aggregation approaches. Our research offers useful insights into how Federalised Learning facilitates collaborative artificial brains that safeguard the privacy of medical data. By creating operational guidelines for healthcare professionals and AI developers who wish to establish large privacy-protecting smart systems, research that relies on real-world emulation acts as the study's basic novelty. In addition to explanatory model solutions for clinical deployment dependability, developers should concentrate on three primary future directions: using blockchain to enable reporting and customising models on device hardware. © 2025 IEEE.
