AB  - Lung cancer remains a leading cause of death worldwide, and reliable subtype identification from computed tomography (CT) is hindered by limited per-subtype data, privacy regulations, and distribution shifts across hospitals. We propose a prototype-based personalized federated learning framework that classifies lung-cancer subtypes directly from lesion-centric CT volumes while preserving privacy. A 3D SEResNet backbone provides embeddings for a prototypical network; prototype similarity between institutions is computed to select the most compatible source model for a requesting site. To protect sensitive information, similarity is computed under Cheon–Kim–Kim–Song (CKKS) homomorphic encryption, and training employs differentially private SGD, yielding quantified privacy without sharing raw images or intermediate features. Using multi-institution CT cohorts from The Cancer Imaging Archive (TCIA), we conduct few-shot, 4-way meta-testing that reflects realistic inter-site heterogeneity. The proposed approach consistently improves over global aggregation baselines, remains robust under practical privacy budgets, and is particularly effective when moderate shots per class are available. These results on the TCIA collections demonstrate a viable path to secure, personalized, and data-efficient lung-cancer subtype prediction, enabling multi-center collaboration without compromising patient privacy. © 2014 IEEE.
