TI  - Federated Learning-Based Healthcare Model Ensuring Secure Transmission Using Cryptographic Technique
PY  - 2025
T2  - International Conference on Intelligent and Innovative Practices in Engineering and Management, IIPEM 2025
DO  - 10.1109/IIPEM65914.2025.11548352
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105042458923?origin=resultslist
AB  - The integration of Internet of Things (IoT) devices in healthcare has led to exponential growth in medical data generation, enabling real-time patient monitoring and intelligent diagnosis. However, transmitting and processing such sensitive data raises serious privacy, security, and ethical concerns. This research proposes a Federated Learning (FL)-based healthcare model combined with cryptographic techniques to ensure secure data transmission and model validation across distributed IoT networks. In this model, patient data remains locally on IoT-enabled medical devices, while only encrypted model updates are transmitted to a central server using homomorphic encryption and Elliptic Curve Cryptography (ECC). The central aggregator then performs secure model aggregation and validation without exposing raw data. Experimental validation conducted using the IoT Healthcare Sensor Dataset (vitals such as heart rate, oxygen saturation, and body temperature) demonstrates a model accuracy of 94.6% with 96.8% encryption efficiency and minimal latency overhead (4.3%). The proposed framework ensures that sensitive patient data never leaves the device in unencrypted form, addressing both data leakage and compliance issues (HIPAA, GDPR). This approach establishes a secure, privacy-preserving, and trustworthy infrastructure for real-time IoT healthcare analytics. © 2025 IEEE.
