TI  - PRISM-FedSeg: enhancing medical image segmentation through self-supervised federated learning with privacy preservation
PY  - 2026
T2  - Biomedical Signal Processing and Control
VL  - 127
C7  - 111202
DO  - 10.1016/j.bspc.2026.111202
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105046357014?origin=resultslist
AB  - Medical image segmentation is crucial for clinical diagnostics and treatment planning, yet it faces challenges such as data privacy, heterogeneity, and limited annotated datasets in decentralized healthcare settings. This paper introduces PRISM-FedSeg, a privacy-preserving self-supervised federated segmentation framework that incorporates Federated Learning (FL), Split Federated Learning (SFL), and Self-Supervised Learning (SSL) to enhance segmentation accuracy and cross-domain generalization without compromising data privacy. PRISM-FedSeg utilizes Masked Image Modeling (MIM) via BEiT and MAE for robust anatomical feature extraction from unlabeled data. The Adaptive Domain-Aware Reweighting Strategy (ADaReS) dynamically adjusts client contributions based on domain divergence, mitigating model drift in non-IID settings. Secure feature aggregation is achieved through Homomorphic Encryption (HE) using the Paillier cryptosystem. Evaluations on the BUSI and TCGA-LGG datasets demonstrate improved segmentation performance, achieving DSC scores of 96.61 ± 0.28% and 87.89 ± 0.28%, respectively, compared with existing baselines. Additionally, external validation on the open-access Breast Lesion Ultrasound Image Database further demonstrated the strong cross-dataset generalization capability of PRISM-FedSeg. ADaReS improved convergence by 18.4%, while HE introduced only 7.6% additional training time. PRISM-FedSeg provides an efficient and privacy-preserving model for robust medical image segmentation in decentralized healthcare environments. © 2026 Elsevier Ltd
