DO  - 10.1109/IACIS65746.2025.11211455
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105023639844?origin=resultslist
AB  - The exponential growth of electronic health records (EHRs) requires robust privacy-preserving frameworks that combine secure storage, encrypted analytics, and regulatory compliance. This paper proposes an optimized permissioned blockchain framework integrated with partially homomorphic encryption (PHE) for healthcare record-keeping. Unlike prior blockchain-HE architectures, our model introduces batch encryption, modulus switching, and parallel computation within Hyperledger Fabric, significantly improving throughput and latency. Experiments on both a synthetic dataset of 50,000 patient records and the real-world MIMIC-III EHR dataset demonstrate predictive accuracy of 97.8% with negligible degradation compared to plaintext computation. Performance analysis shows 142 TPS throughput, 9.4% encryption overhead, and 18% storage savings against hybrid blockchain-storage baselines. The system achieves HIPAA and GDPR compliance, enforces fine-grained access control through smart contracts, and supports FHIR-compliant interoperability across heterogeneous healthcare platforms. Security evaluation confirms resilience against collusion, replay, and side-channel attacks. These results indicate that the framework offers a scalable, secure, and regulation-compliant solution for privacy-preserving healthcare analytics. © 2025 IEEE.
