DO  - 10.1109/SSITCON66133.2025.11342038
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105033152486?origin=resultslist
AB  - The integration of Internet of Things (IoT), Machine Learning (ML), and blockchain has recently gained importance in modern healthcare systems to allow real-time monitoring, secure data sharing, and accurate disease prediction. However, blockchain-enabled Hybrid Deep Learning (HDL) models for healthcare monitoring face major limitations, like high computational cost of heavy Deep Neural Networks (DNN) and privacy-performance trade-off of homomorphic encryption. To overcome these issues, lightweight and privacy-preserving edge framework is proposed, in which patient data are collected from IoT sensors. preprocessing performed through cleaning, normalization, one-hot encoding, and feature extraction. Furthermore, efficient lightweight model known as Lightweight Gradient Boosting was trained with quantization to reduce the memory and computation overhead. For privacy, Federated Learning (FL) ensures that data stay on-device, while Differential Privacy (DP) and Partial Homomorphic Encryption (PHE) secure sensitive fields without heavy overhead. Furthermore, a permissioned blockchain with smart contracts manages secure access, whereas the compressed model is deployed on edge devices for real-time inference, explainability, and secure doctor-patient interaction. The proposed lightweight privacy-preserving healthcare monitoring framework demonstrated improved efficiency, reliability, and strong privacy protection, as well as superior accuracy (95%), precision (94 %), and recall (94 %) compared with the existing blockchain-enabled Hybrid Deep Learning (HDL) approach. © 2025 IEEE.
