TI  - Enhanced privacy-preserving neural networks with fully homomorphic encryption: Optimized search and training
PY  - 2026
T2  - Journal of Information Security and Applications
VL  - 101
C7  - 104543
DO  - 10.1016/j.jisa.2026.104543
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105041255173?origin=resultslist
AB  - The widespread application of deep learning in healthcare has raised critical concerns regarding data privacy and regulatory compliance. Privacy-preserving neural networks (PPNN) based on fully homomorphic encryption (FHE) offer a promising solution by enabling computation on encrypted data. However, existing FHE-based approaches suffer from high computational overhead and significant accuracy degradation, limiting their practical utility. To address these challenges, this paper proposes an optimized PPNN framework that co-optimizes model architecture and training procedure. First, an improved Bayesian optimization method that incorporates an exponential penalty on parameter count into the acquisition function, enabling efficient architecture search for compact and FHE-friendly models. Then, a collaborative training strategy combining Stochastic Weight Averaging (SWA) and Cosine Annealing Learning Rates (CALR), which enhances generalization and inference accuracy in the encrypted domain. By integrating these techniques into the HEMET architecture, our final solution achieves approximately 40% reduction in parameters, 40% shorter inference time, and over 4% improvement in accuracy on CIFAR-10, Cleveland, and OCTID datasets. These results validate the engineering practicality of our approach and contribute to scalable, privacy-preserving medical data analysis. © 2026 Elsevier Ltd.
