TI  - Secure Federated Multimodal Learning with Attention-Weighted Transformers and Homomorphic Encryption for Privacy-Preserving Neuroimaging Analysis
PY  - 2026
T2  - Biomedical Signal Processing and Control
VL  - 123
C7  - 110550
DO  - 10.1016/j.bspc.2026.110550
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105038861346?origin=resultslist
AB  - Neuroimaging technologies such as magnetic resonance imaging (MRI), functional MRI (fMRI), and positron emission tomography (PET) play a critical role in diagnosing and monitoring neurological disorders. However, the growing scale and complexity of multimodal neuroimaging data, combined with the strict privacy requirements in healthcare, pose significant barriers to centralized machine learning approaches. This research work proposes a Federated Multimodal Transformer (FMT) architecture that aims to improve predictive modeling in distributed medical centers while maintaining data privacy. By utilizing attention-weighted fusion mechanisms, the model combines structural MRI (sMRI), resting-state fMRI (rs-fMRI), and diffusion MRI (dMRI) modalities to learn intricate inter-modal interactions. The architecture employs homomorphic encryption (HE) in secure federated learning protocols. Benchmarked on benchmark datasets and compared with recent state-of-the-art models like FedHealthNet, MMFL, and MedFuseFL, the introduced FMT has the best accuracy of 92.80% and AUC of 0.964, surpassing the current methods by as much as 7.4% in accuracy and 5.4% in AUC. These results validate the efficacy and robustness of FMT in processing privacy-sensitive, multimodal neuroimaging tasks. © 2026 Elsevier Ltd
