TI  - Transformer Based Personalized Medicine from Gene Expression Patterns with Privacy Considerations
PY  - 2025
T2  - 2025 5th International Conference on Artificial Intelligence and Signal Processing, AISP 2025
DO  - 10.1109/AISP68263.2025.11396296
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105035736495?origin=resultslist
AB  - The usage of generative deep learning models with personalized medicine offers very promising opportunities for tailoring medical drugs to individual genetic profiles. In this study, we propose a transformer-based model GeneTransMol that synthesizes target drug molecules from genetic expression profile, enhancing molecular validity and diversity. Our approach builds on the Gx2Mol framework by replacing its LSTM-based SMILES generator with a multi-layer transformers architecture capable of capturing long-range dependencies. We use variational autoencoders (VAE) to extract compact gene embeddings from which the transformer generates hit like therapeutic molecules. The transformer model shows faster convergence than LSTM-based methods, its performance plateaus in later stages, indicating potential areas for architectural refinement. Additionally, we discuss privacy-preserving approaches such as data anonymization, homomorphic encryption, and federated learning to protect sensitive genomic data in personalized drug discovery pipelines. This work highlights the viability of transformer-based architectures for precision therapeutics and outlines pathways for secure, privacy-aware model deployment.  © 2025 IEEE.
