AB  - A Federated Edge Learning (FEL) architecture, described in this paper, was developed to facilitate patient monitoring as a scalable, secure and real-time feature. The proposed FEL framework integrates heterogeneous biomedical IoT devices, such as ECG patches, continuous glucose monitor, neurostimulators, and implantable cardiac devices, which can communicate with localized edge gateways and can engage in the federated model training with preprocessing performed on the devices. A hybrid CNN-LSTM classifier is applied to multimodal biosignal classification and considers the constraints of wearable and implantable devices. Privacy preserving technologies such as homomorphic encryption and secure aggregation are used to protect model updates to gradient inversion attacks and model poisoning attacks, and differential privacy enhances data confidentiality. The simulation was based on synthetic multimodal biomedical data (ECG, glucose, and temperature signals) through a comprehensive MATLAB-based simulation to measure FEL performance alongside centralized learning (CL) and cloud-assisted edge learning (CAEL) baselines. Experiments show that FEL outperforms CAEL and CL in average classification accuracy (96.2% versus 91.8% versus 89.7%), yet still performs 36% faster in terms of end-to-end latency and 29% cheaper in terms of communication cost due to on-device feature extraction. The proposed framework further showed adaptive and more trustworthy security with adversarial and privacy-constrained settings, further proving its feasibility in next-generation digital healthcare systems of high priority: Low latency, high accuracy, and privacy compliance. © 2026, Institute of Applied Mathematics of Baku State University. All rights reserved.
