TI  - Parallel and memory-efficient homomorphically encrypted CNNs using vector and tensor representations
PY  - 2026
T2  - Journal of Computational and Applied Mathematics
VL  - 488
C7  - 117797
DO  - 10.1016/j.cam.2026.117797
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105041165882?origin=resultslist
AB  - Homomorphic Encryption (HE) enables computation on encrypted data, providing strong privacy guarantees for sensitive applications such as healthcare, finance, and cloud computing. However, integrating HE into Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs) introduces substantial computational challenges, including high memory usage, multiplicative depth limits, and error accumulation. This work presents a set of parallel and memory-efficient algorithms for encrypted CNN inference using the Residue Number System Cheon-Kim-Kim-Song (RNS-CKKS) scheme. We extend the TenSEAL library with four optimized convolution strategies (Direct, Toeplitz, Lowering, and Rotation). Three methods rely on the CKKSTensor representation, while a novel CKKSVector-based method encodes the entire input in a single ciphertext to reduce memory overhead. All implementations exploit multithreading and SIMD-style slot parallelism, and include optimized matrix multiplication primitives for encrypted linear algebra. These techniques are integrated into privacy-preserving versions of CryptoNets and LoLa, enabling end-to-end encrypted inference for convolutional and fully connected layers. Experimental evaluation on two CPU processors, an Intel Xeon Sapphire, a high-performance server processor, and an ARM Cortex-A78AE embedded in an NVIDIA Jetson Orin, an edge device processor, shows that Rotation excels in single-sample inference, Direct performs best for larger batch sizes, and Toeplitz minimizes memory usage. A detailed error analysis quantifies trade-offs among accuracy, efficiency, and memory, demonstrating the influence of polynomial modulus degree on numerical precision. Overall, vector-based representations combined with parallelism provide a practical and high-performance solution for secure CNN inference on both servers and resource-constrained edge devices. © 2026 The Authors
