TI  - MediChain-FSL: A Blockchain-Integrated Federated Semi-Supervised Learning System for Secure Breast Cancer Diagnosis in Smart Home Healthcare Devices
PY  - 2026
T2  - IEEE Transactions on Consumer Electronics
DO  - 10.1109/TCE.2026.3707898
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105043745480?origin=resultslist
AB  - Breast cancer remains the most commonly diagnosed cancer among women worldwide, with early-stage detection improving five-year survival rates to over 90%. Despite advances in artificial intelligence and consumer electronics for home-based healthcare monitoring, security and privacy concerns remain barriers to widespread adoption for sensitive medical applications like breast cancer diagnosis. Given that access to regular screening remains limited in many communities due to infrastructure and cost constraints, this paper introduces MediChain-FSL, a blockchain-integrated federated semi-supervised learning system for secure breast cancer assessment using smart home healthcare devices and wearable sensors. MediChain-FSL addresses data scarcity and privacy preservation through decentralized learning, where blockchain ensures immutable audit trails and secure multi-party computation while federated semi-supervised learning exploits limited expert-labeled data and abundant unlabeled sensor data from consumer electronics. Our framework employs a blockchain-validated quality-aware aggregation protocol combined with homomorphic encryption and differential privacy mechanisms for consensus-based model aggregation, preventing poisoning attacks and ensuring data integrity across distributed IoT devices, including smart mirrors, thermal imaging systems, and biometric wearables. The semi-supervised component combines consistency regularization under stochastic augmentations with confidence-based pseudo-labeling to exploit abundant unlabeled sensor data. Experimental evaluation using a combined multi-modal dataset of 3,800 samples derived from three distinct data sources spanning clinical, thermal, and biosignal modalities demonstrates 93.4% classification accuracy with 54% communication reduction and sub-180ms latency on resource-constrained devices. The system achieves 98.7% of maximum performance using only 5% labeled data, maintains over 90% accuracy under 20% Byzantine attacks with formal differential privacy guarantees, and successfully detected seven early-stage cancer cases during field deployment across 45 smart home environments. © 1975-2011 IEEE.
