TI  - Memory-Efficient Quantization-Aware Optimization for FHE-Based Mammogram Classification
PY  - 2026
T2  - SysCon 2026 - The 20th Annual IEEE International Systems Conference, Conference Proceedings
DO  - 10.1109/SysCon66367.2026.11503496
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105040686164?origin=resultslist
AB  - Privacy-preserving medical imaging through Fully Homomorphic Encryption (FHE) is critical for secure data processing but is severely constrained by the massive computational overhead of encrypted domain operations. This study evaluates a privacy-preserving medical diagnostic system for mammograms using an optimized, FHE-native Convolutional Neural Network (CNN) and a high-performance compilation environment. We demonstrate a critical precision-latency trade-off: while a high-precision configuration achieves 89.68% accuracy, it necessitates an inference latency of 34 minutes. Conversely, a 3-bit quantized model maintains a suitable accuracy for secondary screening of 86.04% while reducing execution time to just 74.14 seconds. These results characterize the feasibility of 'FHE-first' architectural designs, providing a framework for high-throughput, secure oncological data evaluation. This study investigates the structural and precision-based optimizations required to balance evaluation reliability with the hardware constraints of modern FHE compilers.  © 2026 IEEE.
