AB  - "Federated Learning"is an adaptable approach to model training that enables collaborative Machine Learning (ML) while preserving raw data local, thereby safeguarding privacy and adhering to strict regulatory standards. However, Federated Learning is still susceptible to privacy attacks that extract sensitive details from model updates, such as gradient leakage and property inference. To overcome these issues, various privacy preserving techniques such as Differential Privacy (DP), Homomorphic Encryption (HE), and Hybrid or Integrated approaches were introduced. In Homomorphic Encryption, every calculations done on encrypted information without revealing the raw data. And Differential Privacy will introduce random noise (ϵ) in order to maintain privacy of individual data. Quantitative analysis proves Differential Privacy can reduce accuracy by 15% for each 0.10 drop in ϵ, while Homomorphic Encryption adds 2.30 to 3.50 times the computational overhead, depending on the encryption method. This survey reviews privacy attacks targeting Federated Learning, assesses the benefits and drawbacks of Differential Privacy and Homomorphic Encryption, and examines hybrid strategies that combine these approaches to strengthen privacy protections. Furthermore, it highlights future research directions, such as healthcare, focusing on scalability, efficiency, and resilience against adversarial attacks, aiming to drive progress toward robust system. © 2025 IEEE.
