AB  - Federated learning has proven to be a viable approach that can be used to perform collaborative data mining in the healthcare sector, although strict privacy policies and the threat of data breaches still impede multi-institutional collaboration. Conventional techniques may fail in achieving a trade off between high privacy guarantees, communication efficiency, and model predictability with non-IID data distributions of distributed medical problems. The method presents PrivMedFL, a privacy-preserving federated learning framework based on a hierarchy, which incorporates threshold multi-key homomorphic encryption and adaptive differential privacy to support secure client-edge-cloud training. The architecture facilitates 3D medical image analysis, as well as IoMT cyber threat detection, without the need to share raw patient data. Assessed on the BraTS 2020 dataset and synthetic IoMT tasks, PrivMedFL results in Dice similarity coefficients of 90.1, 88.2, 87.3 whole tumor, tumor core and enhancing tumor regions, respectively, and an F1-score of 0.97 in threat detection, with competitive efficiency and resilience to dropouts and collusion. The findings indicate that PrivMedFL offers a viable solution to the privacy and utility challenge, providing a viable avenue to scalable and secure healthcare data mining. © 2026 IEEE.
