TI  - Adaptive Homomorphic Encryption-Based Tensor Factorization for Edge-Enabled Privacy-Preserving Systems
PY  - 2026
T2  - 2026 International Conference on Frontiers of Engineering and Emerging Technologies, FET 2026
DO  - 10.1109/FET68771.2026.11601583
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105046137035?origin=resultslist
AB  - Modern data-intensive applications such as personalized recommendation systems, IoT analytics, and healthcare prediction rely on multi-dimensional and highly sparse tensors for modelling user-item-context relationships. Ensuring privacy in these settings is a persistent challenge, as Fully Homomorphic Encryption (FHE) enables encrypted computation but incurs heavy computational overhead and noise accumulation. To address these inefficiencies, this paper proposes HSTF- FHE, a Hybrid Sparse Tensor Factorization framework that integrates block-compressed sparse tensor representation, adaptive encryption level control, and SIMD-parallel encrypted computation to achieve secure and scalable tensor learning. The first innovation, the Block-Compressed Sparse Tensor (BCST) structure, transforms high-dimensional sparse tensors into compact encrypted blocks, reducing ciphertext redundancy by 45.1% and communication rounds by 59.1%compared to CSR-FHE. Secondly, the Adaptive FHE Level Control dynamically manages encryption modulus levels and employs lazy bootstrapping, minimizing noise growth and improving computational efficiency by up to 40%. Thirdly, SIMD-based Encrypted Tensor Batch Processing accelerates parallel updates, achieving an overall 2.3× reduction in runtime compared to conventional FHE-based matrix factorization.Experimental evaluations on the MovieLens 100K dataset demonstrate that HSTF-FHE achieves an RMSE of 0.827, maintaining accuracy comparable to plaintext models (0.820) while reducing training time from 472 s (CSR-FHE) to 218 s. The framework also exhibits faster convergence and better noise stability under 32-bit fractional precision, ensuring practical scalability for large, encrypted recommender systems. These results establish HSTF-FHE as a promising step toward privacy-preserving, high-performance tensor learning, suitable for deployment in sensitive domains such as healthcare, finance, and IoT-based personalized analytics. © 2026 IEEE.
