AB  - Brain tumor categorization is a vital step in early detection and treatment planning. Yet, forwarding medical images to cloud models poses serious privacy issues. This paper introduces a privacy-preserving brain tumor classification method using Cheon-Kim-Kim-Song(CKKS) homomorphic encryption and patterned noise injection. The method encrypts preprocessed MRI scans with CKKS for encrypted inference on a Convolutional Neural Network (CNN) without decryption. Gaussian noise is added to the encrypted images to provide additional security, and a denoising process is then performed to retrieve classification accuracy. Encrypted CNN uses im2col transformation, encrypted matrix multiplication, and ciphertext rotation for safe inference. Experimental results show an encrypted test accuracy of 90.61%, highlighting homomorphic encryption's viability for secure medical image classification in research settings. The proposed model serves valuable insights for developing privacy-preserving deep learning models in real-world applications. © 2025 IEEE.
