AB  - The exponential growth of big data in healthcare, finance, and other critical sectors has amplified the need for advanced privacy-preserving data mining (PPDM) techniques. This study presents a comprehensive evaluation of four major PPDM methods - Differential Privacy, Homomorphic Encryption, Data Masking, and Secure Aggregation - by assessing their performance across metrics such as accuracy, latency, utility, privacy score, and computational cost. Special emphasis is given to their application in encrypted electrocardiogram (ECG) signal classification within cloud-based machine learning environments. Through comparative analysis, descriptive statistics, and ANOVA testing, the findings reveal that while Homomorphic Encryption offers the highest level of privacy, it imposes substantial computational overhead. In contrast, lightweight techniques like Data Masking maintain data utility and performance but with lower privacy guarantees. The study also visualizes industry adoption trends and user trust scores, revealing significant trade-offs between confidentiality, usability, and processing speed. The results suggest that no single method satisfies all requirements, making hybrid approaches essential for scalable, privacy-compliant big data analytics in sensitive domains.  © 2025 IEEE.
