AB  - The rapid growth of Internet of Things (IoT) ecosystems in domains such as healthcare, transportation, industrial automation, and smart cities has created an urgent need for secure, efficient, and privacy-preserving communication methods. Conventional cryptographic techniques impose excessive computational, memory, and energy overhead on resource-constrained IoT devices, limiting their practical adoption. This paper presents a hybrid security framework that integrates Federated Learning (FL), Homomorphic Encryption (HE), and Lightweight Cryptography (LWC) into a unified pipeline for privacy-preserving IoT environments. FL enables decentralized training without exposing raw data, HE allows secure computation over encrypted updates, and LWC ensures low-complexity cryptographic operations suitable for constrained devices. Experimental evaluation using the NSL-KDD dataset shows that the proposed FL+HE+LWC framework achieves 91.63% accuracy, an F1-score of 92.72%, and reduces privacy risk to 0.08, with minimal overhead in communication and latency compared to existing solutions. Preliminary validation on the UNSW-NB15 dataset further demonstrates adaptability across diverse IoT benchmarks. The results confirm that combining FL, HE, and LWC enhances communication security, efficiency, and attack resistance, making the framework a novel and practical solution for scalable and privacy-preserving IoT deployments. © 2025 IEEE.
