TI  - Secure and Privacy-Preserving AI: A Zero Trust Architecture for Federated Machine Learning
PY  - 2025
T2  - 2025 IEEE 1st Secure and Trustworthy Cyberinfrastructure for IoT and Microelectronics, SATC 2025 - Conference Proceedings
DO  - 10.1109/SATC65530.2025.11137026
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105017966199?origin=resultslist
AB  - The proliferation of Federated Machine Learning (FML) has opened new avenues for collaborative artificial intelligence (AI) development, enabling multiple parties to train models collectively without exposing sensitive local datasets. However, it introduces significant cybersecurity challenges, particularly in securing data exchanges, model integrity, and system access. A Zero Trust Architecture (ZTA) offers a robust security framework to address these challenges by implementing strict access controls, continuous verification, and minimizing trust zones. This study investigates the implementation of zero-trust principles in AIdriven Federated Machine Learning (FML) environments. It emphasizes the necessity of dynamic authentication, secure communication protocols, and robust defense mechanisms to enhance resilience against adversarial threats. By integrating zero-trust security into FML, organizations can establish a framework to ensure that no user or system is inherently trusted. The main security techniques include cryptographic measures such as homomorphic encryption and secure multiparty computation to safeguard model updates, differential privacy to protect training data, and real-time anomaly detection for threat identification. Additionally, the implementation of FML identities and finegrained policy enforcement enhances the security of user and device authentication across distributed nodes. This study highlights the critical role of the Zero Trust model in mitigating key threats in Federated Machine Learning (FML) systems, including data poisoning, model inversion, and communication interception. Adopting ZTA in FML fortifies security and enhances stakeholder confidence, fostering the broader adoption of federated AI technologies in the healthcare, finance, and defense industries. © 2025 IEEE.
