TI  - PPGNN-E: Efficient Privacy-Preserving GNN Inference via a Modular and Pipelined Arithmetic-and-Logic FHE Accelerator
PY  - 2026
T2  - IEEE Transactions on Computer-Aided Design of Integrated Circuits and Systems
DO  - 10.1109/TCAD.2026.3734393
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105050613392?origin=resultslist
AB  - The rapid growth of cloud-based Graph Neural Network (GNN) inference services raises critical privacy concerns regarding sensitive graph data, such as private node features in patient networks and graph topology in social networks. Although Fully Homomorphic Encryption (FHE) addresses this by enabling computations on encrypted data, utilizing it for privacy-preserving GNN inference incurs significant costs. This is because it generally necessitates ensuring the confidentiality of the highly sparse graph topology—which forces the execution of massive redundant computations in the encrypted domain—and maintaining computational precision, both of which are computationally expensive. Existing FHE-based GNN inference schemes suffer from excessive computational overhead, accuracy degradation, or incomplete data protection. This paper presents PPGNN-E to address all these challenges simultaneously. We first propose a novel privacy-preserving GNN inference algorithm utilizing a high-accuracy arithmetic-and-logic FHE approach. This method employs significantly smaller parameters, thereby substantially reducing computational complexity and facilitating parallel processing. Furthermore, we integrate a Differential Privacy (DP) strategy to efficiently leverage graph sparsity and employ Homomorphic Expansion (H-Exp) to alleviate memory bandwidth bottlenecks. Complementing this, we design a dedicated hardware architecture to implement these innovations, featuring specialized units for arithmetic and logic FHE operations in a pipelined manner. PPGNN-E delivers average speedups of 5.7× and 3.7× over Arithmetic FHE and Logic FHE accelerators, respectively, while maintaining high accuracy and reducing energy consumption by an average of 7.9×. © 1982-2012 IEEE.
