TI  - A Multi-Modal Federated Graph Learning Approach for Health Insurance Pricing with Attention and Explainability on the Cloud
PY  - 2025
T2  - Proceedings of the 2025 3rd International Conference on Cyber Physical Systems, Power Electronics and Electric Vehicles, ICPEEV 2025
DO  - 10.1109/ICPEEV67897.2025.11291437
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105032367591?origin=resultslist
AB  - Current health insurance underwriting practices largely rely on static demographic inputs and manual risk assessment, resulting in limited personalization and potential bias. With the proliferation of wearable devices and mobile health apps, continuous streams of biometric and behavioral data are now available. However, privacy concerns and a lack of interpretability hinder their practical use in premium prediction.This study proposes a novel Federated Graph-Attention Learning (FGAL) framework that integrates privacy-preserving federated learning with secure aggregation, differential privacy, and homomorphic encryption, alongside graph neural networks and SHAP-based interpretability, for dynamic and explainable premium prediction. The system models multi-modal user data-including physical activity, heart rate, BMI, and sleep quality-as temporal graphs to capture intra-feature dependencies, while edge devices train local models using attention-based GNNs. The federated setup avoids raw data sharing, and a centralized aggregator synchronizes encrypted model updates. The model is deployed across AWS edge-cloud infrastructure (SageMaker, EC2, and IoT Greengrass) to support real-time, distributed inference. In experimental evaluation using simulated 90-day behavioral data from 100 users, FGAL achieved an RMSE of 18.7, outperforming centralized DNN (25.4) and XGBoost (27.1). Furthermore, it attained a SHAP fidelity score of 0.85, defined as the correlation between SHAP attributions and model output variations, ensuring reliable interpretability. System overhead, measured as the combined percentage increase in latency and compute cost due to privacy mechanisms, remained below 11%, confirming deployment feasibility.These results validate FGAL as a scalable, accurate, and transparent approach for personalized health insurance premium modeling. © 2025 IEEE.
