AB  - Federated learning (FL) with homomorphic encryption (HE) enables privacy-preserving machine learning (ML) across hospitals without centralizing sensitive health data. This work presents a secure FL framework for collaborative brain tumor classification using public MRI datasets. Each hospital trains a local model, encrypts gradient updates with a trusted HE schema (eg BFV, CKKS) algorithms, and sends the encrypted global model. No raw patient data leaves local sites, preserving confidentiality in compliance with GDPR and HIPAA. We evaluate the proposed framework using public brain tumor MRI datasets and simulate with multiple (3) clients (institutions). Encryption overheads, convergence behavior, accuracy, and communication cost are measured. Our results demonstrate that classification accuracy remains on par with conventional FL while providing robust data privacy guarantees. Computational overhead is manageable with parameter tuning, and communication remains practical across realistic network conditions. We highlight key trade-offs between privacy, utility, and efficiency. The findings show that HE-enabled FL is a viable approach for multi-center medical AI development without exposing sensitive medical images. The proposed FLHE framework for privacy preservation promised the way for scalable, secure collaboration across healthcare institutions, fostering innovation in diagnosis while preserving patient privacy and model performance. © 2025 IEEE.
