AB  - In the present era of attention to data privacy, traditional machine learning (ML) paradigms that rely on centralized data processing are coming under increasing scrutiny. Federated Learning (FL) offers an alternative by allowing for distributed model training and allowing data to remain on local devices while only sharing model updates. This review of the literature has examined how FL can help improve data privacy along with its benefits, challenges, and areas that need to be further explored. It reviewed important privacy-preserving techniques with FL such as differential privacy, secure model aggregation, and homomorphic encryption, along with security threats and attacks posed by adversarial attacks and inference attacks, while noting the need for additional optimization for FL to further help balance privacy, performance, and scalability for FL in privacy-sensitive sectors such as healthcare, finance, and the Internet of Things (IoT). Artificial intelligence is changing the healthcare sector, while also highlighting the issues of data privacy. In this study, we presented federated learning as a privacy-preserving AI technique that allows multiple hospitals to jointly train a model while still preserving their raw data. We used differential privacy, secure aggregation, and homomorphic encryption to enhance security and ensure compliance. This approach represents an ethical AI solution without compromising patient privacy. ©2025 IEEE.
