UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105041083181?origin=resultslist
AB  - The use of deep learning models on endoscopic images for detecting gastrointestinal diseases presents a major privacy challenge, as it requires processing sensitive patient data and risks the unauthorized disclosure of personal health information. To address this issue, we propose SecureEndoNet, a transfer learning-based framework compatible with homomorphic encryption for privacy-preserving endoscopic image classification. SecureEndoNet employs the CKKS scheme to encrypt client data during inference and utilizes a pre-trained VGG-19 model for feature extraction. Experiments conducted on the publicly available KVASIR v2 dataset, which contains eight classes, demonstrate that the framework achieves identical classification accuracy on both plaintext and ciphertext inputs (90.125%), with an inference time of 1.25 seconds per encrypted sample. Moreover, VGG-19 provides the fastest inference speed while achieving over 90% accuracy, outperforming other pretrained models including DenseNet-201, MobileNetV2, ResNet50, and EfficientNetV2B0. © 2025 IEEE.
