C7  - 83
DO  - 10.1145/3807503.3819441
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105046622450?origin=resultslist
AB  - Machine learning holds the potential to improve personalized treatment strategies. Trust remains a key development axis towards adoption, as models have displayed unequal performance across subgroups in related applications. In this study, we investigate ancestry-related fairness, and evaluate alternative training strategies for PAM50 breast cancer subtyping with gene expression data.Using TCGA's BRCA dataset, fairness is assessed regarding White, Black, Hispanic and Asian patients. Transformers, 2D and 1D convolutionnal neural networks, and XGBoost are integrated in this study, containing three training configurations. First, in a centralized baseline setting, disparities across models disfavor Asian and Hispanic samples, and are sustained after equalizing samples. Then, models are trained incorporating ancestry, which only marginally alters model behaviors, although minor improvements are observed for XGBoost and NeXt-TDNN regarding precision. Finally, federated learning, secured via fully homomorphic encryption, is evaluated through two strategies. The first reflects a realistic configuration, where data allocation across centers follows the geographical distribution, while the second uses a randomized distribution. Both setups lead to slightly better global performance, with group-wise results tied to sample distributions. © 2026 Copyright held by the owner/author(s).
