UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105037839659?origin=resultslist
AB  - Federated Learning (FL) enables collaborative medical model training without data sharing but faces challenges from data heterogeneity and privacy constraints. This work uses a hierarchical RLWE-based privacy-preserving FL framework for breast cancer detection using mammography and ultrasound imaging. The framework integrates xMK-CKKS homomorphic encryption to ensure secure model aggregation while analyzing the impact of statistical non-IID conditions (Dirichlet α ∈{1.0,0.5,0.1}) across 2-100 clients. Results indicate stable accuracy and specificity (>84%,>96%), whereas recall exhibits non-monotonic variation across heterogeneity levels, because of uneven malignant sample allocation under Dirichlet-based non-IID partitioning. Regulating client-level class imbalance through controlled label proportioning substantially improved minority class sensitivity and metric stability, yielding significant recall gains with negligible accuracy trade-off. Inclusion of multimodal data improved diagnostic consistency and reduced bias toward either class. Findings emphasize that clinical reliability in federated medical imaging extends beyond accuracy, requiring sensitivity and agreement based evaluation. © 2026 IEEE.
