TI  - Adaptive Federated Learning with Dual Random Masking and Threshold-Based Secure Aggregation for Enhanced Privacy
PY  - 2025
T2  - Proceedings of the 2025 1st International Conference on Advances in Engineering and Computing Technologies for Sustainable Development, AECTSD 2025
DO  - 10.1109/AECTSD65988.2025.11411408
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105036525307?origin=resultslist
AB  - A trilemma concerning an idealized account of federated acquisition (FL) in high-stakes, privacy-sensitive environments limits its potential use in practice due to a trade-off among three key components: data privacy, computational efficiency and theoretical account fidelity. That is, current approaches to federated learning either sacrifice some model performance due to "noise"introduced by differential secret sharing mechanisms or they are computationally intensive, such as with homomorphic encryption. In this paper, we present a novel communication protocol that resolves the trilemma, utilizing adaptive federated learning combined with a dual random masking mechanism that leverages both lightweight chaotic cryptographic random number generators to achieve multiple layers of security, and also utilizes threshold based fix aggregation via Shamir's Secret Sharing to protect against a snooping server. In addition, we demonstrate the efficacy of our proposed approach using a real-world medical imaging problem with a ResNet-18 architecture. Empirical results clearly show that our protocol achieves similar levels of model accuracy to a non-private baseline, provides a strong and statistically demonstrable level of privacy protection, and has low computational overhead. That is, the practical and scalable nature of our approach will enable the first-ever secure and collaborative AI applications in high-stakes, regulated industries. © 2025 IEEE.
