TI  - FedDeepRiskNet++: a privacy-enhanced federated learning framework for secure and scalable medical IoT systems
PY  - 2026
T2  - Cluster Computing
VL  - 29
IS  - 14
C7  - 803
DO  - 10.1007/s10586-026-06616-6
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105051920656?origin=resultslist
AB  - The rapid growth of the Internet of Medical Things (IoMT) has enabled real-time patient monitoring and predictive healthcare analytics, but it has also introduced severe challenges related to data confidentiality, privacy leakage, and secure multi-institution collaboration. Conventional federated learning (FL) frameworks preserve data locality; however, they remain susceptible to gradient leakage, inference attacks, communication overhead, and client heterogeneity in distributed hospital environments. This paper proposes FedDeepRiskNet++, a privacy-enhanced FL framework for secure and scalable medical IoT systems. The proposed framework integrates four complementary modules: differential-privacy-based local perturbation, CKKS homomorphic-encryption-based secure aggregation, adaptive privacy-budget scheduling (APBS), and adaptive gradient sparsification with encryption-aware communication packaging. The APBS dynamically balances model utility, privacy preservation, and resource efficiency according to client reliability, energy status, and communication conditions. Extensive experiments using the PhysioNet 2023 and MIMIC-III datasets demonstrate that FedDeepRiskNet + + achieves 91.2% classification accuracy and 90.1% F1-score while maintaining a low privacy budget (). Compared with FedAvg, DP-FedAvg, PAHFL, Health-FedNet, and the original FedDeepRiskNet, the proposed framework reduces communication cost by approximately 23%, lowers cumulative communication overhead to 165 MB, and achieves faster convergence under heterogeneous non-IID hospital environments. Additional ablation analysis confirms that APBS significantly improves the privacy-utility trade-off compared with static privacy-budget allocation. The results demonstrate that strong privacy protection, communication efficiency, and high diagnostic reliability can coexist within large-scale federated healthcare systems, making FedDeepRiskNet + + a practical solution for next-generation medical IoT systems. © The Author(s), under exclusive licence to Springer Science+Business Media, LLC, part of Springer Nature 2026.
