AB  - With the increasing demands for privacy protection in cross-domain medical coordination and related fields, the Paillier homomorphic encryption algorithm has garnered significant attention as a foundational technology for data privacy preservation. In large-scale data scenarios such as disease prediction models in major hospitals, cross-bank financial risk prediction, and inter-regional health monitoring statistics, substantial ciphertext computations are required. Consequently, traditional Paillier schemes encounter the following limitations. To enhance encryption computational performance, this paper proposes a multi-prime decomposition-based Paillier scheme that overcomes the deficiency of existing optimization methods being solely applicable to decryption procedures. The advantage lies in parallelizing the two primary modular exponentiation operations gm and in Rn the encryption process without algorithmic modifications, subsequently computing the final ciphertext through an improved CRT construction algorithm. The technical implementation adopts a staged reconstruction mechanism to mitigate overflow risks in large-number arithmetic, incorporating cryptographically secure random number generation and balanced prime allocation strategies to ensure coordinated unification between computational load and security strength. Algorithmic analysis demonstrates that under sufficient parallel conditions, near-linear acceleration performance can be achieved. Experimental validation shows that our method achieves 2-4 × performance improvement under 2048-bit key configurations, providing efficient cryptographic support for practical deployments such as medical federated learning and secure multi-party computation.  © 2025 IEEE.
