TI  - Privacy-Preserving Multi-Modal Learning for CKD Progression Prediction Using Federated Clinical Data
PY  - 2026
T2  - 2026 International Conference on Emerging VLSI Semiconductor Technology for AI and Computing Applications, EVST 2026
DO  - 10.1109/EVST69093.2026.11660540
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105049166437?origin=resultslist
AB  - Chronic Kidney Disease (CKD) are affecting more than 850 million people within the world. Early prediction remains difficult because clinical records, laboratory biomarkers, retinal imaging, and clinical notes are often fragmented across institutions and cannot be centrally pooled because of privacy regulations. This research presents a privacy-preserving federated learning framework for CKD progression prediction that is designed to integrate longitudinal clinical biomarkers, retinal imaging, and clinical text without raw data sharing. The framework uses attentionbased multimodal fusion, quality-aware client selection, pseudo-modality generation for missing inputs, differential privacy, and homomorphic encryption. To avoid overclaiming from unpaired public datasets, the evaluation is reported as a federated simulation and comparative benchmark using MIMIC-III/MIMIC-IV clinical data, OCTA-500/RFMiD retinal data, and performance values from related studies. The proposed benchmark configuration reports an internal AUC of 0.91 and external AUC of 0.86, while the fully attention-fused model achieves precision of the 0.94, recall of 0.95, F1-score of 0.945, and AUC of 0.97 in the ablation comparison. Under non-IID federated settings, FedMRI-style aggregation improves convergence compared with FedAvg, reaching 70.1% accuracy in the simplified 4-class setting. Adaptive differential privacy preserves most model utility, with 91.7% Dice score compared with a 92.9% non-private baseline. The study should therefore be interpreted as a privacy-preserving framework and benchmark analysis rather than a fully paired clinical-imaging validation study. © 2026 IEEE.
