C7  - 132
DO  - 10.1007/s13721-026-00773-9
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105041480079?origin=resultslist
AB  - The current healthcare system that relies on big data requires medical professionals to maintain proper medical data authentication and data integrity to achieve precise disease diagnosis and conduct distance patient treatment. The existing centralized systems, together with their black-box learning models, fail to provide adequate security measures and privacy safeguards and interpretability, limiting their clinical adoption. The research introduces a reliable healthcare analytics system that improves data protection and model visibility while enabling efficient processing of healthcare systems at an extensive scale. The framework combines three technologies that enable secure data sharing through blockchain technology while protecting user identity and applying an advanced Homomorphic Proxy Re-Encryption Scheme with Strong Anti-Collusion (HPRE-SAC) solution. The system incorporates federated learning (FL) through its differential privacy function and uses Explainable Artificial Intelligence (XAI) techniques for its automatic decision support system, which creates personalized treatment plans. The system was tested using three types of medical data: genetic data, MRI images, and electronic health records (EHR). The HPRE-SAC framework shows better results than all current encryption and optimization methods through its experimental testing. The system achieves encryption times of 0.19876s and 0.19863s and decryption times of 0.20984s and 0.21477s and system turnaround times of 0.4086s and 0.431s while maintaining restoration efficiency of 0.99162 and 0.99218. The proposed framework improves data security and privacy protection and interpretability of results, while computational speed, which makes it ideal for use in healthcare analytics systems that require both trustworthiness and scalable operations, enabling secure and explainable clinical decision-making. © The Author(s), under exclusive licence to Springer-Verlag GmbH Austria, part of Springer Nature 2026.
