AB  - The digitization of the healthcare system has been high and has, in turn, led to the extensive use of Electronic Health Records (EHRs), which have provided better clinical decisions, patient care, and medical research. However, privacy and security of EHR data are quite delicate, in particular, when using machine learning (ML) models, which require access to huge amounts of data. Privacy-preserving machine learning (PPML) is a new paradigm that can assist in addressing these problems, in which sensitive patient information is secure and valuable insights can be derived. This review consists of the description of the existing technologies in PPML of EHRs, including federated learning, differential privacy, homomorphic encryption, and secure multiparty computation. We look at their merits, weaknesses and the ways to incorporate them in real situations of health care. Besides, we highlight the following prominent points: scalability, computational overhead, trade-offs in the model accuracy and compliance with regulatory standards such as HIPAA and GDPR. Emerged trends, including blockchain-based privacy technologies and hybrid solutions, are also stated. By summarizing the existing trends, this paper will guide researchers and health care practitioners to adopt effective PPML practices that would guarantee a reasonable use of data and patient confidentiality, which in the long run will guarantee the establishment of safe and trustful AI-based healthcare models.  © 2026 IEEE.
