TI  - Privacy-Preserving Federated Learning for Heart Disease Prediction—A Review of Security Architectures and Techniques
PY  - 2026
T2  - Lecture Notes in Networks and Systems
VL  - 1984 LNNS
SP  - 69
EP  - 82
DO  - 10.1007/978-3-032-31216-7_7
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105047085039?origin=resultslist
AB  - Heart disease remains the leading cause of mortality worldwide, necessitating collaborative research across healthcare institutions to develop accurate prediction models. Traditional centralized machine learning approaches face significant barriers due to patient privacy concerns, regulatory constraints, and institutional reluctance to share sensitive medical data. Federated learning emerges as a promising solution that enables collaborative model training while maintaining data locality and privacy protection. This paper presents a comprehensive review of privacy-preserving federated learning techniques specifically applied to heart disease prediction, examining security architectures and their effectiveness in healthcare environments. We systematically analyze differential privacy, homomorphic encryption, and secure multi-party computation approaches, evaluating their security guarantees, computational overhead, and clinical utility preservation. Our comparative analysis reveals that differential privacy provides the most practical solution for large-scale healthcare collaborations, achieving 85–95% of centralized learning accuracy while maintaining formal privacy guarantees. The review identifies significant implementation challenges including regulatory compliance complexities, technical deployment barriers, and inter-institutional coordination requirements. We highlight critical research gaps in scalability, real-world deployment strategies, and integration with existing healthcare infrastructure. Our findings provide actionable guidance for healthcare institutions considering federated learning adoption while identifying essential directions for future research in privacy-preserving collaborative healthcare analytics. © The Author(s), under exclusive license to Springer Nature Switzerland AG 2026.
