TI  - Quantum-Inspired Feature Selection and Federated Intelligence for Predictive Geriatric Healthcare
PY  - 2026
T2  - Proceeding - 2026 IEEE 6th International Conference on Computing, Power, and Communication Technologies, IC2PCT 2026
SP  - 844
EP  - 849
DO  - 10.1109/IC2PCT68894.2026.11584337
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105045301306?origin=resultslist
AB  - As the elderly population continues to grow, there is an urgent need for decentralized, privacy-preserving, and high-accuracy predictive healthcare models that can proactively manage chronic illnesses. Most traditional Federated Learning (FL) approaches often have challenges with non-IID data distribution, slow convergence rates, the potential for security attacks, and high communication cost. To capitalize on the benefits of FL while addressing limitations, we propose a Quantum-Inspired Federated Learning (QIFL) framework that combines quantum computing concepts with FL to improve predictive performance, security, and computational efficiency. The QIFL model utilizes Quantum Variational Circuits (QVCs) and Quantum-Inspired Stochastic Gradient Descent (QSGD) to expedite local model updates, while Quantum Differential Privacy (Q-DP) guarantees patient data protection. In addition, the framework employs a Quantum Gate-Based Feature Selection (QGFS) mechanism to identify informative biomarkers for early detection of age-related diseases such as cardiovascular Diseases, Alzheimer's and Diabetes. Additionally, a Quantum-Inspired Adaptive Federated Averaging (QAFedAvg) method is proposed to optimize the aggregation of the global model from heterogeneous healthcare nodes. The framework also uses Quantum Secure Homomorphic Encryption (Q-SHE) and Quantum-Key Distribution (QKD) as security and privacy approaches to prevent adversarial model poisoning attacks during a federated update. The QIFL framework is evaluated on real-world elderly healthcare datasets (MIMIC-III, eICU, and PhysioNet) and performance is based on prediction accuracy, convergence time, adversarial robustness, and privacy leakage resistance. The QIFL framework demonstrates superior performance over standard FL frameworks, achieving a prediction accuracy of $97.8 \% (\pm 0.3 \%)$, a convergence time of $\mathbf{5 2}$ percent quicker, a $\mathbf{4 1 \%}$ lower adversarial success rate, and a reduced communication cost by 42%. This work reveals the effectiveness of quantum inspired computing and federated learning methods to produce an accurate, secure, and computationally efficient predictive healthcare model for the elderly population. The authors will continue to explore future work using Quantum Neural Networks (QNN) to enhance predictive intelligence and real-time diagnostics. © 2026 IEEE.
