TI  - Retina-Mesh: A Reconfigurable Silicon-Immunity Architecture for Federated Vision
PY  - 2026
T2  - IEEE Access
VL  - 14
SP  - 112681
EP  - 112697
DO  - 10.1109/ACCESS.2026.3705226
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105043008499?origin=resultslist
AB  - Federated learning (FL) enables collaborative model training across hospitals without sharing patient data, yet model updates remain vulnerable to privacy attacks that can reconstruct sensitive medical images. This paper introduces Retina-Mesh, a hardware-software co-designed architecture that enforces patient privacy at the silicon level. Our key insight is that by executing all privacy-critical computations - feature extraction, attention modeling, and feature fusion - directly on reconfigurable FPGA hardware, we eliminate the software-side attack surfaces exploited by membership inference and model inversion attacks. The architecture operates in three stages: (1) a local objectness detector identifies diagnostically important regions without exposing raw images; (2) a secure attention mechanism collaboratively models radiologist gaze patterns across institutions under homomorphic encryption; and (3) an on-chip hypergraph module fuses high-order anatomical features entirely within the FPGA's secure boundary. Differential privacy with strict guarantees (ϵ =0.5, δ =10-5 ) is enforced at the aggregation stage. Evaluated on 12,000 CT scans across 10 simulated hospitals, Retina-Mesh reduces membership inference attack accuracy to near-random levels (52.3%) and increases model inversion error by 8.6×, while preserving 93.5% of diagnostically critical regions at 24.1 ms latency and 19.5 W power consumption. These results demonstrate that hardware-enforced privacy can meet the simultaneous demands of security, diagnostic accuracy, and real-time clinical performance.  © 2013 IEEE.
