TI  - MKXGB: Privacy-preserving XGBoost training over encrypted multi-source data via multi-key homomorphic encryption
PY  - 2026
T2  - Computer Standards and Interfaces
VL  - 98
C7  - 104160
DO  - 10.1016/j.csi.2026.104160
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105033078880?origin=resultslist
AB  - The effective integration of multi-sourced data is crucial for enhancing decision-making capabilities in complex systems such as intelligent healthcare and finance. The XGBoost model, known for its high precision, is an ideal model for such fused data. However, privacy regulations and risks restrict the centralized use of multi-party data. To overcome this issue, we propose MKXGB, a secure, non-interactive XGBoost training scheme based on multi-key homomorphic encryption (MKHE), which can safely integrate encrypted multi-sourced data with the model training process. MKXGB allows multiple users to encrypt their data locally with their private keys. Subsequently, the Cloud Server (CS) can complete the model training directly on the aggregated ciphertexts without decryption or frequent interactions. We have designed secure algorithms for core operations such as gradient and gain computation. A key advantage of MKXGB is its unified support for both horizontal and vertical data partitioning within a single framework, allowing it to adapt to diverse multi-source data scenarios. Furthermore, users can go offline after a single round of data upload, supporting dynamic participation. Experiments show that MKXGB maintains accuracy comparable to plaintext training while ensuring strong privacy protection and reducing communication overhead, providing possibilities for future applications in privacy-sensitive scenarios. © 2026 Elsevier B.V.
