TI  - Secure Federated Deep Learning for Breast Cancer Detection: Enhancing Privacy with Obfuscation and Multi-Modal Intelligence
PY  - 2026
T2  - Proceedings of the 2026 International Conference on Intelligent and Innovative Technologies in Computing, Electrical and Electronics, IITCEE 2026
DO  - 10.1109/IITCEE67948.2026.11394239
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105041721604?origin=resultslist
AB  - Among women’s health disorders, breast cancer ranks as one of the most prevalent and life-threatening diseases. However, privacy regulations and distributed clinical data restrict the use of centralized deep learning systems. A Secure Federated Deep Convolutional Neural Network (FDCNN) is developed in this research to facilitate breast cancer detection among different medical centers without compromising patient confidentiality. The proposed system integrates Differential Privacy (DP), Homomorphic Encryption (HE), and feature-space obfuscation to ensure secure parameter transmission and resistance against inference attacks. The architecture leverages multi-modal medical imaging—including mammography, histopathology, and ultrasound—to improve diagnostic generalization. Experimental evaluations on benchmark datasets under simulated federated conditions demonstrate a classification accuracy of 93.7% and an AUC of 94.9%, closely approximating centralized learning performance while reducing privacy leakage risk by 70%. The results validate that the proposed privacy-enhanced FDCNN achieves high diagnostic efficacy with strong data confidentiality, establishing its potential for deployment in HIPAA- and GDPR-compliant healthcare environments. ©2026 IEEE.
