AU  - Wan, Y.
TI  - Computing-Empowered E-Health and Telemedicine: System Architecture, Core Algorithms, and System Challenges - Submission Template for ACM Papers
PY  - 2026
T2  - Proceedings of 2026 2nd International Conference on Health Informatization and Data Analysis, HIDA 2026
SP  - 295
EP  - 302
DO  - 10.1145/3819113.3819163
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105048668514?origin=resultslist
AB  - The rapid convergence of advanced computing paradigms with e-health and telemedicine has precipitated an urgent need for scalable, secure, and intelligent remote healthcare infrastructures - particularly in the context of global digital transformation, escalating chronic disease burdens, and demographic shifts toward aging populations. This paper presents a comprehensive, system-level investigation into computing-empowered telemedicine, grounded in the interdisciplinary nexus of health informatics, medical artificial intelligence, and distributed systems engineering. A unified, layered microservices-based architecture is proposed, integrating heterogeneous Health Internet of Things (H-IoT) sensing modalities at the edge with cloud-native orchestration layers, FHIR-compliant interoperability mechanisms, and clinical application interfaces - designed to ensure deployment agility, fault tolerance, and cross-institutional workflow continuity. Complementing this architectural foundation, the paper critically surveys and synthesizes core algorithmic enablers: federated learning frameworks adapted to non-independent-and-identically-distributed (non-IID) clinical data streams, multimodal data fusion techniques that reconcile imaging, time-series physiological signals, unstructured clinical narratives, and genomic profiles under uncertainty-aware alignment constraints, and privacy-preserving computation methods - including homomorphic encryption, zero-knowledge proofs, and trusted execution environments - that uphold regulatory compliance without compromising analytical fidelity. System-level challenges are rigorously categorized across three dimensions: technical-operational impediments such as ultra-low-latency requirements for telesurgery, energy-constrained edge inference, and model drift induced by dynamic patient cohorts; regulatory-ethical gaps including auditability deficits in GDPR/HIPAA-aligned AI deployments, explainability mandates for FDA-cleared clinical decision support tools, and liability attribution complexities arising from distributed microservice failures; and foundational trust deficits related to demographic bias propagation, adversarial vulnerability in federated settings, and absence of standardized provenance frameworks. By articulating actionable design principles, algorithmic trade-offs, and interdisciplinary research trajectories - including neuro-symbolic clinical reasoning, blockchain-augmented data lineage, self-supervised pretraining for label-scarce modalities, and consensus-driven federation governance - the work bridges theoretical advances with pragmatic implementation pathways for healthcare providers, technology developers, and policy stakeholders alike. © 2026 Copyright held by the owner/author(s).
