AU  - Zhang, P.
AU  - Lu, H.
AU  - Ding, T.
TI  - Homomorphic activation and argmax via bootstrapping and applications in privacy-preserving neural networks
PY  - 2025
T2  - Neurocomputing
VL  - 651
C7  - 130862
DO  - 10.1016/j.neucom.2025.130862
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105010678751?origin=resultslist
AB  - Privacy-preserving neural networks (PPNN) aim to provide inference as a service without breaching users’ privacy, which have many potential applications in the smart healthcare field. One promising technology to realize PPNN is fully homomorphic encryption (FHE), which supports homomorphic operations over the encrypted data. However, FHE-enabled PPNN is facing two major challenges. First, most FHE schemes only support arithmetic operations, but not non-linear operations (e.g., ReLU, Argmax). Second, most FHE-based PPNN schemes avoid using bootstrapping for the sake of efficiency, which, however, significantly limits the depth of neural networks. In order to execute non-linear operations over the encrypted data and extend them to neural networks with arbitrary depth, we adopt a bit-wise fully homomorphic encryption scheme (i.e. TFHE with bootstrapping), so that we are able to realize non-linear homomorphic activation functions and argmax directly. First, based on the gate bootstrapping and programmable bootstrapping in TFHE, Full-Domain Programmable Bootstrapping(FD_PBS) is realized, which provides a framework for computing arbitrary linear and non-linear functions in bootstrapping. Next, non-linear homomorphic activation functions are achieved by designing the corresponding test polynomial in FD_PBS, and non-linear homomorphic argmax function is advanced by homomorphic comparison using gate bootstrapping. Last, based on homomorphic activation and argmax, privacy-preserving neural networks with arbitrary depth are proposed, which yield high efficiency by performing activation during bootstrapping instead of running an extra activation layer. Experiments show that we can homomorphically evaluate non-linear activation and argmax directly; homomorphic activation enables the inference accuracy over the ciphertext to be close to that over the plaintext; and homomorphic argmax improves inference time by returning only the class with the largest predicted probability rather than all classes and their probabilities to the user. © 2025
