SP  - 827
EP  - 833
DO  - 10.1109/ISRITI68345.2025.11393406
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105035740129?origin=resultslist
AB  - The rapid digital transformation in healthcare has accelerated the adoption of Electronic Medical Records (EMRs), yet concerns about privacy, interoperability, and security remain major challenges. Centralized learning models expose data to leakage and adversarial attacks, reducing clinical reliability. To address these issues, this study proposes a Secure Federated Learning (FL) framework integrated with Blockchain to enable scalable, privacy-preserving, and trustworthy EMR management. A quantitative experimental design was conducted using the publicly available MIMIC-III dataset in a simulated multi-institutional environment. Federated learning enabled local model training across distributed nodes, while blockchain served as a permissioned ledger for update verification, node authentication, and access control. Homomorphic encryption and differential privacy were incorporated to enhance confidentiality. System performance was evaluated in terms of predictive accuracy, calibration, communication efficiency, and security resilience. The proposed framework achieved an average prediction accuracy of 94.7%, with balanced precision (93%) and recall (92%), and an AUROC of 0.97. Calibration also improved, yielding lower Brier and ECE values compared with baselines. Communication latency remained practical at 210 ms per iteration, while bandwidth consumption decreased by 18% relative to vanilla FL. Security analysis showed that the system detected anomalous updates under poisoning attacks with an 87% success rate, while differential privacy reduced membership-inference risk toward chance levels. The blockchain layer further ensured transparency and auditability of all transactions without degrading predictive utility. In conclusion, integrating federated learning with blockchain provides a scalable, auditable, and privacy-preserving solution for EMR management. This framework advances secure digital healthcare infrastructures and establishes a foundation for real-world deployment in hospital ecosystems. © 2025 IEEE.
