DO  - 10.1109/ICBDS67396.2025.11377403
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105034419884?origin=resultslist
AB  - Providing privacy for data in healthcare analytics is a major challenge, especially when handling sensitive patient data. This paper introduces a privacy-preserving machine-learning framework utilizing Cheonimim Song (CKKS) Homomorphic Encryption to provide secure computation on encrypted medical data without divulging plaintext values. The proposed approach employs logistic regression to predict potential health risks with end-to-end encryption throughout the whole process. For additional protection of data in transit, a second level of Advanced Encryption Standard - Galois/Counter Mode (AES- GCM) encryption is applied. The experimental outputs demonstrate that the accuracy of the encrypted model is the same as the accuracy of the non-encrypted one, which confirms that homomorphic encryption may allow to do secure machine learning without significant performance degradation. Homomorphic encryption may increase the computation load, though; batch encoding and cipher text packing make it more efficient. These findings lead to the possibility of machine learning in encrypted medicine, and more research can be done to create safe medical AI systems.  © 2025 IEEE.
