SP  - 1111
EP  - 1116
DO  - 10.1109/EICCT69950.2026.11564512
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105043787661?origin=resultslist
AB  - To address the issues of vulnerable data tampering, weak identity authentication mechanisms, and the difficulty in balancing encryption efficiency and security in On-Board Diagnostics (OBD) devices, this paper proposes a tamper-resistant two-way authentication method for OBD systems. The method first collects OBD device data and vehicle sensor data to complete cloud registration; then classifies data into critical/non-critical categories based on the driving safety impact threshold, and implements hierarchical protection using symmetric encryption and approximate homomorphic encryption respectively; generates dual-entropy dynamic salt values by combining ciphertext time entropy and device operation entropy, and completes two-way authentication through cloud reverse reconstruction and challenge-response mechanism; finally generates permission tokens for access control based on authentication results. Experiments show that this method effectively guarantees data authenticity and integrity, resists replay and forgery attacks, and improves authentication security while meeting the low-latency requirements of the Internet of Vehicles (IoV). © 2026 IEEE.
