TI  - EdgeSeal-FHE: A Privacy-Preserving Encryption Framework for End-to-End Encrypted Health Monitoring
PY  - 2026
T2  - Procedia Computer Science
VL  - 280
SP  - 363
EP  - 370
DO  - 10.1016/j.procs.2026.04.047
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105042436378?origin=resultslist
AB  - The increasing use of the Internet of Things (IoT) and wearable devices in healthcare exposes sensitive patient data to security risks when transmitted through untrusted networks. In this paper, we present EdgeSeal-FHE, an end-to-end encryption framework that applies Fully Homomorphic Encryption (FHE) at the edge to ensure that physiological data remain encrypted from generation to cloud analysis with no plaintext exposure. The system uses the Cheon-Kim-Kim-Song (CKKS) scheme via TenSEAL to support computation on encrypted medical embeddings, enabling continuous confidentiality and integrity without disrupting real-time processing. Evaluation results show that EdgeSeal-FHE encrypts 300-dimensional embeddings in 4.52 ms at 128-bit security, achieves end-to-end latency of 35.48 ms, and preserves 99.72% data quality with less than 0.5% reconstruction loss. Compared with state-of-the-art CKKS healthcare systems, our approach delivers higher throughput while remaining under the 100 ms real-time threshold across all device classes, including low-end smartphones. These results demonstrate that practical, real-time encrypted healthcare monitoring is achievable without sacrificing security, accuracy, or responsiveness.  © 2026 The Authors.
