SP  - 56
EP  - 61
DO  - 10.1109/ICIRCA69024.2026.11570642
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105044112686?origin=resultslist
AB  - Healthcare systems produce huge volumes of sensitive patient information, such as medical records, diagnostic images, and real-time sensor information. Conventional centralized machine learning methods demand centralization of this sensitive information under a central server to train on, and this has huge privacy, security, and regulatory issues, including HIPAA and GDPR compliance. Another promising method is Federated Learning (FL), which allows training models in a collaborative mode among distributed healthcare organizations and stores patient data on a local level. The data exchanged are only model updates and not raw data, thus minimizing the risks of privacy. In this paper, a thorough overview of privacy threats exists in healthcare FL, gradient leakage, membership inference, and model poisoning attacks. It also looks at privacy-preserving methods including Differential Privacy (DP), Homomorphic Encryption (HE), Secure Multi-party Computation (SMPC), and Trusted Execution Environments (TEE). Moreover, it suggests a new privacy-saving FL model that suits resource-limited healthcare IoT settings such that patient information remains secret without affecting the model efficiency. Trade-offs in terms of privacy guarantees, diagnostic accuracy, and computational efficiency are shown through experimental evaluation. The suggested framework provides a way towards safe, scalable, and privacy-sensitive AI in health care. © 2026 IEEE.
