TI  - AI-Enhanced Privacy Preservation Using Homomorphic Federated Models
PY  - 2025
T2  - 2025 1st International Conference on Advancement in Futuristic Technologies, ICAFT 2025
DO  - 10.1109/ICAFT66710.2025.11453096
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105037322341?origin=resultslist
AB  - The explosive growth of intelligent edge devices has motivated the desire to have privacy sensitive machine learning models that can securely share knowledge without letting the raw data leak out. The conventional federated learning (FL) models reduce the risks of central data aggregation but still can be susceptible to inference, reconstruction, and model inversion attacks because of unencrypted gradient exchange. The paper suggests an AI-Enhanced Homomorphic Federated Model (AE-HFM) to combine the concept of fully homomorphic encryption (FHE) with adaptive privacy-aware optimization to be able to perform secure computations on encrypted model parameters. The framework uses encrypted gradient aggregation, differential-noise reinforcement, and lightweight key-rotation protocol to minimize the possibility of leakage without compromising the performance of learning. Also, an AI-based optimization controller balances the encryption overhead, communication frequency, and computation budget of heterogeneous nodes dynamically. Experimental analysis of benchmark healthcare and smart-city datasets, experimental results show that AE-HFM can offer privacy resilience through inference attacks, on par with state-of-the-art FL approaches, and with only less than 9% computational cost. These findings affirm that the solution suggested is an important step in the right direction in terms of providing privacy-sensitive distributed learning applications with high confidentiality, scalability and robustness. © 2025 IEEE.
