DO  - 10.1109/ICRISET64803.2025.11252516
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105031377692?origin=resultslist
AB  - Securing wireless communication in heterogeneous Wireless Sensor Networks (WSNs) remains a critical challenge due to the diversity of node capabilities and constrained resources. This paper introduces an Optimized Key Distribution Protocol (OKDP) designed to ensure secure, energy-efficient, and scalable data transmission in such heterogeneous environments. The methodology integrates node classification into high-capacity cluster heads and low-power sensing nodes, followed by identity-based lightweight authentication. A Firefly Optimization Algorithm is used over a matrix-based key pre-distribution approach, with minimum memory overhead and maximum key-sharing likelihood. Forward and backward secrecy is achieved by use of dynamic key establishment through elliptic curve Diffie-Hellman (ECDH) and hash-based rekeying. The protocol is also reinforced by the fact that a trust-aware intrusion detection module is in place isolating compromise nodes in real time. The proposed OKDP achieved an average key sharing probability of 94.18%, compromised node tolerance of 85.26%, energy consumption of 1.45 J, and an operational accuracy of 96.42% across various simulation scenarios. Its significantly higher performance in resilience, energy consumption, and communication overhead are confirmed by comparative results against well-known benchmark protocols (LEAP+, ECC-KPS, SPINS, etc.). The privacy-sensitive characteristics of the privacy-preserving data aggregation are secured through the adoption of the homomorphic encryption, which does not hinder the computational tractability. OKDP architecture is highly scalable and flexible and could therefore be well suited to the IoT present-day use cases such as smart farming, combat surveillance, and medical supervision. This paper, therefore, proposes a secure and effective protocol that is lightweight, less CPU intensive and optimized to support real-time secure transmission of data in WSNs composed of heterogeneous nodes.  © 2025 IEEE.
