AB  - Evaluation and diagnosing are critical in managing the security and efficiency of any cryptographic systems. Correct assessments assist in defining the threats and strengthening the system, which plays a critical role today when information is at the forefront. In this context, the diagnostic approaches should also be continually refined in response to the dynamics in the cryptographic method to match arising threats and new scenarios of attack. Maintaining a high degree of precision and productivity in these assessments does not only enhance the security of cryptographic systems but also speeds up their utilization in important applications. Current techniques, like VHC-CIES, PSO, IND-CPA, and CryptoSolve, have drawbacks, including the lack of accuracy in intricate analyses and decreased efficiency in real-world scenarios. Herein, we propose the 'CrypTest' diagnostic framework for CKKS-Based Homomorphic Cryptosystems (CKKS-HCs) by employing the Deep Spiking Neural Coupling Network (DS-NCNet) and Aphid Ant Optimization (A2O). The framework follows a three-phase analysis: Ciphertext Analysis, Key Estimation and Behavioural Driven Modelling. After analysis, A2O further adjusts the structure of DS-NCNet for optimal performance. CrypTest increases the accuracy, speed, and reliability of diagnoses compared to previous methods due to its advantages. Observing the results, the excellent enhancements in the performance parameters where accuracy values are above 98.5%, encryption security levels are over 98% and computational efficiency enhancements are above 95%. These metrics define the effectiveness of the proposed tool - CrypTest - as a reliable and accurate means of CKKS-HC assessment. © 2024 IEEE.
