TI  - FMLEE: Federated Meta-Learning with Enhanced Encryption for Secure Collaborative Training
PY  - 2024
T2  - 2024 15th International Conference on Computing Communication and Networking Technologies, ICCCNT 2024
DO  - 10.1109/ICCCNT61001.2024.10724680
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85211183375?origin=resultslist
AB  - Federated Learning (FL) trains models on distributed devices by aggregating model updates or parameters from multiple clients, enhancing data privacy by keeping data local. However, FL still poses risks of inversion attacks and potential malicious behavior by aggregators. Homomorphic encryption (HE) and other privacy-preserving techniques, while necessary, can be vulnerable to attacks such as adversarial attacks, membership inference attacks, and model inversion attacks by clients with access to the HE private key. This paper introduces Federated Meta-Learning with Enhanced Encryption (FMLEE), a novel framework that integrates HE, symmetric session key encryption to secure multi-party computation. FMLEE allows for collaborative training and refining of a meta-learning model without disclosing sensitive data and model weights, even without completely trusting the aggregator. HE safeguards model weights, enabling the aggregator to perform aggregation without accessing the actual data. Symmetric key encryption secures communication between the aggregator and clients, protecting model weights from being exposed to other clients as well. This dual-layer encryption approach ensures data security during both computation and communication, effectively addressing passive and active security threats. By incorporating meta-learning, FMLEE facilitates the development of robust models with less data. The use of symmetric session keys ensures the secure transmission of model weights without requiring trust in the communication channel. Extensive experiments demonstrate that FMLEE preserves data and model parameter privacy with minimal efficiency loss. This sets a new benchmark for secure federated learning by balancing privacy, security, and performance, making FMLEE highly applicable in sensitive domains such as healthcare, finance, and national security.  © 2024 IEEE.
