DO  - 10.1109/ICIC68258.2025.11413011
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105035367688?origin=resultslist
AB  - The detection of anomalies is a foundational component of modern security and risk management systems. Traditional anomaly detection approaches lack built-in mechanisms for data privacy and accountability. This paper presents a privacy-protected anomaly detection system that combines Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs) with blockchain technology to ensure secure and trustworthy surveillance monitoring. The system identifies unusual or suspicious activities in video frames, using data from the UCSD Pedestrian Dataset. To protect privacy, the video frames are encrypted using AES-128. The CNN is trained to accurately classify each encrypted frame as either normal or anomalous. After classification, the results, along with the hash of the encrypted frame and the timestamp, are stored on a private Ethereum blockchain using smart contracts. This step ensures that the results are not only secure but also cannot be altered which provides traceability and transparency. The proposed system achieved over 92% accuracy and an AUC score of 0.91. It also showed low latency and acceptable performance for real-time use. The blockchain component added minimal overhead while ensuring data security and auditability. Lastly, this research offering a solution that combines artificial intelligence with secure data handling. The system is especially useful for applications in smart surveillance, healthcare monitoring, and critical infrastructure, where both accuracy and data privacy are essential. ©2025 IEEE.
