UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85195076520?origin=resultslist
AB  - This study presents a novel technique to protect medical image data from security risks under 6G wireless technology-based communications called FUZET—Fuzzy Encryption for medical image data. This innovative approach combines homomorphic encryption with the Fuzzy Analytic Hierarchy Process (FAHP) and technique for Order Preference by similarity to the Ideal Solution (TOPSIS) framework to improve the security assessment of 6G web-based image processing systems for handling medical image data. Additionally, the important combination of AI-assisted XG boost algorithm improves the entire security risk identification process by using machine learning techniques. This research aims to overcome the critical challenge of identifying risks related to medical imaging (MRI), CT, X-ray and ultrasonic images, particularly in the context of 6G wireless sensor networks. To prevent incorrect diagnoses, protecting these extremely private and sensitive medical picture data from unauthorised access is essential. Thus, a key concern in the healthcare industry is medical imaging security. Even with today’s improved attack types, technologies like 6G, which uses ultra-high-speed low latency to transport data at very high speeds, still face challenges. Combining the decision-making concepts of (FAHP and TOPSIS) and AI technology of XG boost with encryption techniques, the proposed study provides a systematic and rigorous assessment of security risks from various domains, such as data integrity, confidentiality, accessibility and compliance with regulatory standards. The usage of 6G wireless sensor networks allows continuous transmission and exchange of data, and the proposed AI-based technology improves the efficiency and accuracy of security risk identification processes. Additionally, this study highlights the significance of the research in addressing the pressing need for robust security measures in medical image processing systems. It highlights the ability of FUZET to reduce security threats and safeguard sensitive healthcare data. © The Author(s), under exclusive licence to Springer Science+Business Media, LLC, part of Springer Nature 2024.
